1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:17,317
We're here today
with Uhm Jung-hwa and Sooyoung.

2
00:00:17,384 --> 00:00:18,651
Jung-hwa is someone

3
00:00:18,718 --> 00:00:21,688
who's known as Korea's Madonna.

4
00:00:21,755 --> 00:00:24,758
When the chefs heard
Jung-hwa would be joining us,

5
00:00:24,824 --> 00:00:26,426
they said, "Our star is really coming?"

6
00:00:26,593 --> 00:00:27,961
WE'VE BEEN WAITING

7
00:00:28,028 --> 00:00:29,062
-Really?
-Also,

8
00:00:29,129 --> 00:00:31,097
Ho-young said he had trouble sleeping?

9
00:00:31,164 --> 00:00:33,299
-Really?
-I was in the military in 1996,

10
00:00:33,366 --> 00:00:34,734
when a military-themed show

11
00:00:34,801 --> 00:00:37,904
-called <i>Stage of Friendship</i> was on air.
<i>-Stage of Friendship.</i>

12
00:00:37,971 --> 00:00:40,006
Jung-hwa had visited back then and sang

13
00:00:40,073 --> 00:00:41,474
"Love That Only Heaven Allows."

14
00:00:41,541 --> 00:00:42,976
I successfully finished my service

15
00:00:43,043 --> 00:00:44,844
thanks to that performance.

16
00:00:44,911 --> 00:00:46,379
Really? In 1996?

17
00:00:46,446 --> 00:00:48,815
I was a bit disappointed
because I was expecting "Pupil,"

18
00:00:48,882 --> 00:00:50,650
but you sang a ballad instead.

19
00:00:50,717 --> 00:00:52,152
So it kind of killed the vibe.

20
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:57,290
You were the original star,
popular among soldiers.

21
00:00:57,357 --> 00:00:59,426
-Yes, the troops' favorite.
-She was so popular.

22
00:00:59,492 --> 00:01:01,227
I performed often at military camps.

23
00:01:01,294 --> 00:01:03,296
There were times when the stage collapsed.

24
00:01:03,496 --> 00:01:05,031
GASP

25
00:01:05,098 --> 00:01:07,167
I was quite popular.

26
00:01:07,233 --> 00:01:09,202
Of course.
We're all too familiar with that.

27
00:01:09,269 --> 00:01:10,570
She's not Korea's Madonna.

28
00:01:10,637 --> 00:01:13,239
Madonna is America's Uhm Jung-hwa.

29
00:01:13,473 --> 00:01:15,175
HOW SWEET♥

30
00:01:15,809 --> 00:01:17,243
PLEASED…

31
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:21,314
I came to Seoul in 1991,

32
00:01:21,381 --> 00:01:24,918
and a singer named Uhm Jung-hwa
debuted in 1993.

33
00:01:26,052 --> 00:01:28,488
I feel like I kind of know her

34
00:01:29,055 --> 00:01:30,223
like a hometown friend.

35
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:31,624
Where are you from?

36
00:01:31,691 --> 00:01:33,026
I lived in Jecheon for three years.

37
00:01:33,093 --> 00:01:36,229
-Really?
-Yes, I lived there for three years.

38
00:01:36,296 --> 00:01:39,199
Actually, my older sister
was in the same homeroom class

39
00:01:39,265 --> 00:01:41,968
-as Jung-hwa in middle school.
-Really?

40
00:01:42,969 --> 00:01:44,204
REALLY?!

41
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:45,572
-That's why…
-No way.

42
00:01:45,638 --> 00:01:48,842
…I have heard
a lot about her from my sister.

43
00:01:48,908 --> 00:01:50,343
She said, "Jung-hwa has to be big."

44
00:01:50,643 --> 00:01:51,644
MOVED

45
00:01:51,711 --> 00:01:54,981
-Uhm Jung-hwa.
-Uhm Jung-hwa.

46
00:01:55,048 --> 00:01:58,351
I must've somehow felt that.
I always loved seeing you on TV.

47
00:01:58,418 --> 00:02:00,753
-I'm her direct junior.
-You are.

48
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:02,088
-Whoa.
-All right, Sis.

49
00:02:02,155 --> 00:02:03,189
All right.

50
00:02:03,590 --> 00:02:05,725
FRIENDLY HOMETOWN FRIENDS FROM JECHEON

51
00:02:05,792 --> 00:02:09,863
Jung-hwa now has many younger singers
who came after her.

52
00:02:09,929 --> 00:02:11,498
So as you associate with them,

53
00:02:11,564 --> 00:02:14,567
do you ever feel the generational gap?

54
00:02:14,634 --> 00:02:16,836
-Not really.
-No?

55
00:02:16,903 --> 00:02:19,172
-Is it easy to talk to them?
-Yes.

56
00:02:20,273 --> 00:02:22,008
<i>What's it like in your shoes, Sooyoung?</i>

57
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:23,176
Well…

58
00:02:23,843 --> 00:02:26,446
She's right. I never feel that gap.

59
00:02:26,513 --> 00:02:27,947
You mean me?

60
00:02:28,014 --> 00:02:29,482
-No, me.
-Oh, you.

61
00:02:31,451 --> 00:02:32,452
Me, ma'am.

62
00:02:34,187 --> 00:02:36,422
At times, I find myself
calling her like a friend.

63
00:02:36,489 --> 00:02:39,692
-She's that chill?
-Then Jung-hwa shouldn't have a problem.

64
00:02:39,759 --> 00:02:41,995
I'd be like, "Girl, that's hilarious!"

65
00:02:43,029 --> 00:02:44,931
That means she's really chill.

66
00:02:44,998 --> 00:02:46,466
We received some intel.

67
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:47,967
-You did?
-Yes.

68
00:02:48,034 --> 00:02:52,839
We heard you were often seen
eating out with younger colleagues.

69
00:02:52,906 --> 00:02:56,409
When I'm scheduled
to go out and eat with her,

70
00:02:56,476 --> 00:02:58,178
I have to set aside a lot of time.

71
00:02:58,244 --> 00:02:59,846
-I see.
-I see.

72
00:02:59,913 --> 00:03:02,081
-She goes for rounds two and three.
-It's not just lunch.

73
00:03:02,148 --> 00:03:04,017
These days, Gen Z kids will go home

74
00:03:04,083 --> 00:03:05,351
-when you ask them to come.
-Yes.

75
00:03:05,418 --> 00:03:06,819
They feel a lot of pressure.

76
00:03:06,886 --> 00:03:08,154
-They don't like it.
-Really?

77
00:03:08,221 --> 00:03:10,156
And never ask,
"What do you do when you're free?"

78
00:03:10,223 --> 00:03:11,357
-Really?
-Apparently.

79
00:03:11,424 --> 00:03:12,659
I kept asking them.

80
00:03:12,725 --> 00:03:13,726
You did?

81
00:03:13,793 --> 00:03:15,361
I'd ask, "Are you free tomorrow?"

82
00:03:15,428 --> 00:03:17,430
-We aren't supposed to ask.
-We aren't?

83
00:03:17,497 --> 00:03:19,032
-Apparently so.
-You shouldn't.

84
00:03:19,098 --> 00:03:20,433
No calls.

85
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,903
We recently went to dinner

86
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:26,105
and that day, Sooyoung said,

87
00:03:26,172 --> 00:03:28,641
"Let's go to a karaoke room!"

88
00:03:28,708 --> 00:03:30,143
Do singers do that too?

89
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:31,578
I was thrilled that she asked.

90
00:03:31,644 --> 00:03:32,645
JELLY…

91
00:03:32,712 --> 00:03:35,215
-Did a medley of Jung-hwa's songs.
-And Girls' Generation.

92
00:03:35,281 --> 00:03:37,584
-A medley of Girls' Generation songs.
-And ours.

93
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:39,786
It'd take an hour just to do that.

94
00:03:39,852 --> 00:03:41,788
You're right.

95
00:03:41,854 --> 00:03:43,823
You had fun that day, right?

96
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:45,491
It was so much fun, Jung-hwa!

97
00:03:47,193 --> 00:03:49,128
HER SOCIAL MODE IS AT FULL THROTTLE

98
00:03:49,195 --> 00:03:50,330
That's so awkward.

99
00:03:50,396 --> 00:03:51,864
Let's do that again sometime.

100
00:03:52,198 --> 00:03:54,100
I'D LOVE TO♥

101
00:03:54,167 --> 00:03:57,503
What about you, Eun-yeong?
You must know many older chefs.

102
00:03:57,570 --> 00:03:59,038
It happens from time to time.

103
00:03:59,105 --> 00:04:03,376
Actually, it happened rather often
when I worked with Chef Lu Chinglai.

104
00:04:03,443 --> 00:04:05,578
-So?
-Do I make people feel like that too?

105
00:04:05,645 --> 00:04:07,914
No, that wasn't what I meant.

106
00:04:07,981 --> 00:04:09,148
I loved it.

107
00:04:09,215 --> 00:04:12,085
I always went
because I was grateful for the invite.

108
00:04:12,151 --> 00:04:13,286
What if you don't want to go?

109
00:04:13,353 --> 00:04:14,654
When I didn't feel like it,

110
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:17,857
I often said I wasn't feeling well.
That I was under the weather.

111
00:04:17,924 --> 00:04:19,559
-You're never sick.
-You must begin

112
00:04:19,626 --> 00:04:22,528
staging your condition an hour before,
like wiping your sweat.

113
00:04:22,595 --> 00:04:24,564
Start the act from the morning.

114
00:04:24,631 --> 00:04:26,599
"Why am I so feverish today?"

115
00:04:26,666 --> 00:04:28,801
Here's something I do.

116
00:04:28,868 --> 00:04:29,902
I keep on yawning.

117
00:04:30,436 --> 00:04:31,471
YAWN

118
00:04:31,537 --> 00:04:33,840
Like you're saying, "I'm tired."

119
00:04:33,906 --> 00:04:35,608
-Like that day?
-"Jung-hwa…"

120
00:04:35,675 --> 00:04:36,609
Like that day?

121
00:04:36,676 --> 00:04:38,511
-She remembers.
-You remember when she yawned?

122
00:04:38,578 --> 00:04:39,979
She remembers that day.

123
00:04:40,046 --> 00:04:41,347
So we all do that.

124
00:04:41,648 --> 00:04:44,651
SOOYOUNG, YOU MUST'VE BEEN TIRED ^^

125
00:04:45,752 --> 00:04:48,454
Sung-jun and Nam-no,
you're both the Gen MZ.

126
00:04:48,521 --> 00:04:50,590
-Yes.
-What if older chefs did this?

127
00:04:50,657 --> 00:04:52,325
We're kind of different from each other.

128
00:04:52,392 --> 00:04:53,726
Yes, Nam-no shows up often.

129
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:55,094
-I…
-He joins us.

130
00:04:55,161 --> 00:04:57,297
-I show up to these gatherings.
-He does.

131
00:04:57,363 --> 00:04:58,464
Even when I'm sick to death.

132
00:04:59,699 --> 00:05:01,000
Because you love to eat.

133
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:02,935
-Yes, and that's a good thing.
-Wait.

134
00:05:03,002 --> 00:05:05,004
Sung-jun never says no.

135
00:05:05,071 --> 00:05:06,839
He shoots right home without answering.

136
00:05:06,906 --> 00:05:08,007
RUNS WITHOUT A RESPONSE

137
00:05:08,074 --> 00:05:09,142
I don't pick up my phone.

138
00:05:09,208 --> 00:05:10,877
That's right, he doesn't.

139
00:05:10,943 --> 00:05:12,712
That's why he's Gen MZ.

140
00:05:12,779 --> 00:05:17,317
I can only add it to my schedule
if you give me a week's notice.

141
00:05:17,383 --> 00:05:18,885
-A week?
-A week before?

142
00:05:18,951 --> 00:05:21,521
I can't show up
to these spontaneous gatherings.

143
00:05:22,455 --> 00:05:25,224
Jong-won has something

144
00:05:25,291 --> 00:05:29,562
he wants to share with Jung-hwa
to help her communicate with Gen MZ.

145
00:05:29,629 --> 00:05:31,097
<i>You work with many of them, right?</i>

146
00:05:31,164 --> 00:05:33,833
<i>Yes, my entire staff is Gen MZ,</i>

147
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:36,235
so it's important
that I understand their language.

148
00:05:36,302 --> 00:05:38,471
These days, the "ya" meme is on the rise.

149
00:05:38,738 --> 00:05:40,406
YA?

150
00:05:40,973 --> 00:05:43,509
-The first one is <i>yareu.</i>
<i>-Yareu.</i>

151
00:05:43,576 --> 00:05:44,777
That's ancient.

152
00:05:44,844 --> 00:05:46,012
<i>-Yareu.</i>
-It's been a while.

153
00:05:46,079 --> 00:05:47,313
It's already ancient?

154
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:48,414
It's outdated.

155
00:05:48,481 --> 00:05:49,549
YAREU: LIKE "WOOHOO" OR "SLAY"

156
00:05:49,615 --> 00:05:51,117
It's a bit old.

157
00:05:51,184 --> 00:05:55,154
-There's another one, city name and <i>yaho.</i>
<i>-Yaho.</i>

158
00:05:55,221 --> 00:05:57,090
<i>-Yaho.</i>
-That too.

159
00:05:57,357 --> 00:05:59,392
THAT'S OLD TOO…

160
00:05:59,459 --> 00:06:01,260
Not like what you say it
on top of a mountain? "<i>Yaho</i>"?

161
00:06:01,327 --> 00:06:04,263
-It's not what you say when hiking.
-No?

162
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:05,398
It's not this <i>yaho.</i>

163
00:06:05,465 --> 00:06:06,566
KNOWS MORE THAN YOU THINK

164
00:06:06,632 --> 00:06:09,001
I heard it just this morning.

165
00:06:09,068 --> 00:06:11,070
-You did?
-I went to get my makeup done

166
00:06:11,137 --> 00:06:14,440
and the younger staff members there
told me about it.

167
00:06:14,507 --> 00:06:16,542
I said, "Isn't it '<i>yaho</i>'?"

168
00:06:16,609 --> 00:06:17,877
-You went through this.
-Yes.

169
00:06:17,944 --> 00:06:19,011
It's the hikers' "<i>yaho.</i>"

170
00:06:19,078 --> 00:06:21,381
-At the top of Buramsan Mountain.
-But it wasn't that.

171
00:06:21,447 --> 00:06:23,549
-Buramsan Mountain?
-Instead of that,

172
00:06:23,616 --> 00:06:25,151
you still say the same thing

173
00:06:25,218 --> 00:06:26,986
with a nasal voice.

174
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:30,490
NASAL VOICE GALORE

175
00:06:30,757 --> 00:06:31,991
JUST LIKE THIS

176
00:06:32,492 --> 00:06:34,560
MOVED

177
00:06:34,627 --> 00:06:38,197
It's something Japanese <i>gyaru</i> girls said,
and it recently came back.

178
00:06:38,264 --> 00:06:39,932
"Geoje, <i>yaho.</i>"

179
00:06:39,999 --> 00:06:42,135
You just do it without any context?

180
00:06:42,201 --> 00:06:44,771
-It's a greeting. When you meet someone.
-A greeting?

181
00:06:44,837 --> 00:06:46,739
-That's the end of the meme.
-Let's try it…

182
00:06:46,806 --> 00:06:48,408
-…with our hometowns.
-Jinjeop-eup.

183
00:06:48,474 --> 00:06:50,276
-Jinjeop-eup, go first.
-Jinjeop-eup.

184
00:06:50,343 --> 00:06:52,078
Jinjeop-eup, <i>yaho.</i>

185
00:06:52,145 --> 00:06:54,447
TRENDY

186
00:06:54,514 --> 00:06:56,582
-Great job.
-He's good.

187
00:06:56,649 --> 00:06:58,418
Jecheon, <i>yaho.</i>

188
00:06:58,484 --> 00:06:59,852
Jecheon, <i>yaho.</i>

189
00:06:59,919 --> 00:07:01,354
IMMEDIATELY USES WHAT THEY LEARNED

190
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:04,357
Let's go with the meme from our day.
It's great to see you.

191
00:07:04,424 --> 00:07:05,825
Hi, hi.

192
00:07:05,892 --> 00:07:07,326
OF COURSE WE KNOW THAT

193
00:07:07,393 --> 00:07:08,694
-Hey!
-Homegirl!

194
00:07:08,928 --> 00:07:10,463
SPRINGS TO HER FEET

195
00:07:10,630 --> 00:07:12,331
UP AND DOWN

196
00:07:12,398 --> 00:07:13,866
-That's it.
-I've seen it in a drama.

197
00:07:14,167 --> 00:07:17,069
HAVE FUN LIKE WE'VE GONE BACK IN TIME!

198
00:07:17,136 --> 00:07:18,638
We should be using that.

199
00:07:18,704 --> 00:07:20,206
FEELS RIGHT AT HOME

200
00:07:20,273 --> 00:07:24,577
The biggest reason why Jung-hwa thinks
she gets along with younger colleagues

201
00:07:24,644 --> 00:07:28,381
is because of her
extraordinary ability to empathize.

202
00:07:28,448 --> 00:07:32,919
She always hears out people's troubles
with all her heart.

203
00:07:32,985 --> 00:07:34,554
I'm just a good listener.

204
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:36,522
I think that's important.

205
00:07:36,589 --> 00:07:38,057
-I see.
-And after they're done,

206
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:39,425
you invite them to a team dinner.

207
00:07:39,492 --> 00:07:41,727
-We can talk as we eat.
-As they eat.

208
00:07:41,961 --> 00:07:43,763
IT'S FUN FOR EVERYONE, RIGHT?

209
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:46,232
She's like Oh Eun-young, the psychiatrist,
to her colleagues.

210
00:07:46,466 --> 00:07:47,867
I'M PARK EUN-YEONG

211
00:07:47,934 --> 00:07:49,402
So we prepared something.

212
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:52,572
Our chefs were in need of advice,
so they asked anonymously.

213
00:07:52,638 --> 00:07:54,574
That sounds like a lot of fun.

214
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:57,777
-She's very empathetic.
-Can I really empathize with you?

215
00:07:57,844 --> 00:08:01,347
Please hear them out,
empathize with them, and provide advice.

216
00:08:01,414 --> 00:08:03,449
Here's the first story.

217
00:08:03,516 --> 00:08:06,152
"I feel more at home
on the show these days,

218
00:08:06,219 --> 00:08:08,054
so at times, people show a great response

219
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:11,691
-to something random I say."
-Great response?

220
00:08:11,757 --> 00:08:14,594
"Does this mean I can be funny too?"

221
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:16,796
Someone who isn't funny must've said that.

222
00:08:16,863 --> 00:08:20,066
-"I felt confident and began to chime in…"
-I think I know.

223
00:08:20,132 --> 00:08:22,935
"…but I become really down
when people don't laugh."

224
00:08:23,002 --> 00:08:24,537
I get it. This is so true.

225
00:08:24,604 --> 00:08:27,173
-Is it Jong-won?
-"Especially so

226
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,609
when I look at Jung-hwan
after saying something."

227
00:08:29,675 --> 00:08:32,778
"Because I can tell exactly
that what I said wasn't funny."

228
00:08:33,045 --> 00:08:34,647
WHO SAID THIS, WHO?

229
00:08:34,714 --> 00:08:38,251
He's a cynic, so he has
a high standard for jokes.

230
00:08:38,317 --> 00:08:39,719
But he'd laugh at most things.

231
00:08:39,785 --> 00:08:42,054
"After that, I feel so small."

232
00:08:42,989 --> 00:08:45,525
"What am I supposed to do?"

233
00:08:45,591 --> 00:08:47,226
I guess he's a bit timid.

234
00:08:47,293 --> 00:08:51,297
When you begin to feel small,
it can last a long time.

235
00:08:51,364 --> 00:08:53,432
So don't look at Jung-hwan.

236
00:08:54,867 --> 00:08:56,502
Instead, keep chiming in.

237
00:08:56,569 --> 00:08:58,271
-One of them will be funny.
-Don't look?

238
00:08:58,337 --> 00:09:01,507
-Make jokes for yourself.
-For himself.

239
00:09:01,574 --> 00:09:02,675
But he scolds me.

240
00:09:02,742 --> 00:09:03,743
-Yeah?
-He tells me to stop.

241
00:09:03,809 --> 00:09:04,644
Ignore that too.

242
00:09:04,710 --> 00:09:06,312
Yes, you should ignore it.

243
00:09:06,379 --> 00:09:09,248
-Was it Jong-won?
-It was you?

244
00:09:09,315 --> 00:09:10,750
-Was it you?
-It was you?

245
00:09:10,816 --> 00:09:11,651
Oh, gosh.

246
00:09:12,118 --> 00:09:14,654
A MAN WHO WANTS TO BE FUNNY

247
00:09:14,987 --> 00:09:17,423
A MAN WHO JUST WON'T LAUGH

248
00:09:17,490 --> 00:09:19,292
I feel bad for my dear Chef Jong-won.

249
00:09:19,358 --> 00:09:21,093
Even during the last shoot,

250
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:22,562
he said, "Enough is enough."

251
00:09:22,628 --> 00:09:24,697
-"Enough is enough."
-I said that?

252
00:09:24,764 --> 00:09:27,600
I'll use tenderloin
to let you sound tender.

253
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,202
WILL JUNG-HWAN LAUGH AT THIS?

254
00:09:30,269 --> 00:09:31,938
He threw in a joke.

255
00:09:32,004 --> 00:09:33,739
INDIFFERENT…

256
00:09:33,806 --> 00:09:37,076
Excuse me. You're trying too hard.

257
00:09:37,143 --> 00:09:40,212
I think this is the first time
I'm speaking today.

258
00:09:40,279 --> 00:09:42,615
That was why he wasn't talking.

259
00:09:42,682 --> 00:09:44,250
-He was sitting like this.
-Right.

260
00:09:44,317 --> 00:09:45,484
DISHEARTENED

261
00:09:45,551 --> 00:09:46,786
A host should laugh at every joke.

262
00:09:46,852 --> 00:09:49,722
-Of course. He's a host, not an athlete.
-Right?

263
00:09:49,789 --> 00:09:51,357
I'll just keep smiling.

264
00:09:52,391 --> 00:09:53,859
It looks great on you.

265
00:09:53,926 --> 00:09:55,127
Laugh more often.

266
00:09:55,194 --> 00:09:57,129
It wasn't funny,
so how was I supposed to laugh?

267
00:09:57,196 --> 00:09:59,398
It's not like it's a crime.

268
00:09:59,465 --> 00:10:01,367
It's worse to force out laughter…

269
00:10:01,434 --> 00:10:03,636
I also don't find everything funny!

270
00:10:03,903 --> 00:10:05,571
IN SHREDS

271
00:10:05,638 --> 00:10:06,939
-Okay?
-It wasn't funny.

272
00:10:07,006 --> 00:10:09,375
-Is this a crime?
-Just laugh a bit more.

273
00:10:09,442 --> 00:10:10,876
Fine. I'll keep smiling.

274
00:10:10,943 --> 00:10:13,813
Keep smiling
until he builds up enough tolerance.

275
00:10:13,879 --> 00:10:17,416
-There's one last story.
-Another one?

276
00:10:17,483 --> 00:10:20,052
"I love to eat almost too much,

277
00:10:20,119 --> 00:10:22,588
so I know when I should stop,
but it's hard to do so."

278
00:10:22,655 --> 00:10:25,491
"When I see food before me,
my throat tickles."

279
00:10:25,791 --> 00:10:28,694
IT EVEN TICKLES?

280
00:10:29,161 --> 00:10:31,030
"When I eat eggs in the green room

281
00:10:31,097 --> 00:10:33,032
to lose weight, my appetite kicks in."

282
00:10:33,099 --> 00:10:36,035
-His appetite kicks in?
-"So I end up eating <i>ramyeon,</i>

283
00:10:36,102 --> 00:10:38,971
toast, and two lunch boxes."

284
00:10:39,038 --> 00:10:41,807
-It's probably Jung or Yoon.
-Yes.

285
00:10:41,874 --> 00:10:43,442
What should he do?

286
00:10:43,509 --> 00:10:46,078
-He should eat.
-And overcome it? No?

287
00:10:46,145 --> 00:10:48,648
That's what makes him happy.

288
00:10:49,515 --> 00:10:51,884
It would be great to start with eggs,

289
00:10:51,951 --> 00:10:55,121
but I don't want you to lose
that endearing quality,

290
00:10:55,187 --> 00:10:56,355
Mr. Nam-no.

291
00:10:57,490 --> 00:10:59,125
It might not be me.

292
00:10:59,392 --> 00:11:02,395
THE MAN BEHIND THE STORY
SOMEHOW GOT EXPOSED

293
00:11:02,461 --> 00:11:04,230
-He said this too.
-What?

294
00:11:04,296 --> 00:11:07,433
"I even won against a GLP-1 drug."

295
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:09,735
-That's incredible.
-He's something.

296
00:11:09,802 --> 00:11:12,071
I've been trying to cut down on dinner,

297
00:11:12,138 --> 00:11:14,407
and someone told me to try eating eggs.

298
00:11:14,473 --> 00:11:15,374
That's the trend.

299
00:11:15,441 --> 00:11:19,045
But I ended up eating 12 eggs in total.

300
00:11:19,111 --> 00:11:20,579
Once I begin, I can't stop.

301
00:11:20,646 --> 00:11:21,947
Were you at a chicken coop?

302
00:11:22,014 --> 00:11:24,583
Some may feel motivated by you

303
00:11:24,650 --> 00:11:27,086
and want to stay in shape like you,

304
00:11:27,153 --> 00:11:29,088
but the thing is, it may not happen.

305
00:11:29,155 --> 00:11:32,391
What would you say to those people?

306
00:11:32,458 --> 00:11:34,527
-It's so hard.
-It is?

307
00:11:34,593 --> 00:11:37,430
You have to try, though reluctantly.

308
00:11:37,496 --> 00:11:39,231
It's all about what you eat.

309
00:11:39,298 --> 00:11:41,567
It's not the advice itself.

310
00:11:41,634 --> 00:11:43,135
It's the way she talks to you.

311
00:11:43,202 --> 00:11:45,137
She looks into your eyes

312
00:11:45,204 --> 00:11:47,506
with her inner brows raised like this.

313
00:11:47,573 --> 00:11:50,242
-So it's already comforting.
-You're right.

314
00:11:50,309 --> 00:11:53,245
-Really?
-It feels like it's resolved. That's it.

315
00:11:53,312 --> 00:11:55,314
She might ask you
to stay for a group dinner.

316
00:11:55,381 --> 00:11:56,782
Really?

317
00:11:56,849 --> 00:11:59,085
I'd love to go.

318
00:12:00,186 --> 00:12:01,954
Can you karaoke for three hours?

319
00:12:02,021 --> 00:12:05,157
-I love going to a karaoke room!
-He'll stay for rounds two and three.

320
00:12:05,458 --> 00:12:07,493
STAGING HER ACT

321
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:09,628
STAGING HERS TOO

322
00:12:09,695 --> 00:12:11,897
I'm sure the secret
behind how she stays in shape…

323
00:12:11,964 --> 00:12:15,835
-Let's open her fridge.
-…is all in her fridge.

324
00:12:15,901 --> 00:12:19,071
I heard the secret
to her youthful look is inside.

325
00:12:19,138 --> 00:12:21,607
Keep a close eye on it.

326
00:12:21,674 --> 00:12:23,843
When she gets health checkups,

327
00:12:23,909 --> 00:12:26,779
her body age comes out
as someone in her 20s or 30s.

328
00:12:26,846 --> 00:12:27,947
-Yes.
-Right?

329
00:12:28,013 --> 00:12:29,482
I'll be six years old soon.

330
00:12:31,050 --> 00:12:32,818
Her time flows backwards.

331
00:12:33,185 --> 00:12:34,887
HAS NO RESPONSE…

332
00:12:35,521 --> 00:12:37,957
Sorry. I just throw things out there.

333
00:12:38,023 --> 00:12:40,493
-Let's see what she has.
-I want to see it.

334
00:12:40,559 --> 00:12:42,228
Let's take a look at Jung-hwa's fridge.

335
00:12:42,294 --> 00:12:46,065
-What's the secret to her youthfulness?
-What is it?

336
00:12:46,132 --> 00:12:47,967
We present Jung-hwa's fridge!

337
00:12:48,033 --> 00:12:49,201
-Here we go.
-My gosh.

338
00:12:49,769 --> 00:12:51,637
WHOA

339
00:12:51,704 --> 00:12:53,372
We present Jung-hwa's fridge!

340
00:12:56,675 --> 00:12:59,145
HER FRIDGE IS TOTALLY YAREU

341
00:12:59,211 --> 00:13:01,080
And you live alone.

342
00:13:01,147 --> 00:13:04,150
-You have a lot of stuff. I knew it.
-She does.

343
00:13:04,216 --> 00:13:05,718
That's a lot.

344
00:13:05,785 --> 00:13:07,019
Were you surprised, Sooyoung?

345
00:13:07,086 --> 00:13:09,054
-I was.
-You didn't think she'd have this much?

346
00:13:09,121 --> 00:13:11,457
Do you not find grocery shopping tedious?

347
00:13:11,524 --> 00:13:14,460
Whenever I have time, I like to go

348
00:13:14,527 --> 00:13:15,928
to the farmers' market.

349
00:13:15,995 --> 00:13:17,296
-Farmers' market?
-Yes.

350
00:13:17,363 --> 00:13:19,064
-Farmers' market?
-With actual farmers?

351
00:13:19,131 --> 00:13:22,001
-Yes, you've probably heard of Marche.
-Yes, Marche.

352
00:13:22,067 --> 00:13:24,503
It's put together
to support small farmers…

353
00:13:24,570 --> 00:13:25,971
What now?

354
00:13:26,038 --> 00:13:27,239
-Wait.
-What now?

355
00:13:27,306 --> 00:13:29,475
-Don't be mad. Hear him out.
-He said something.

356
00:13:29,542 --> 00:13:30,976
-Listen to what he says.
-What now?

357
00:13:31,043 --> 00:13:33,379
I was commenting.
I wasn't trying to be funny.

358
00:13:33,445 --> 00:13:35,848
-I wanted to hear you out.
-Tell us.

359
00:13:35,915 --> 00:13:39,351
It's called Marche, something that
opens three to four times a month.

360
00:13:39,418 --> 00:13:43,522
<i>Farmers gather to let consumers come</i>
<i>to buy produce directly from them.</i>

361
00:13:43,589 --> 00:13:45,391
<i>-So it's not open every day?</i>
<i>-No.</i>

362
00:13:45,457 --> 00:13:49,395
And they also have vegetables
and fruits with small flaws.

363
00:13:49,461 --> 00:13:50,963
With lower commercial value.

364
00:13:51,030 --> 00:13:54,834
-There's a market that sells those too.
-But it's good.

365
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:58,337
I like to get my produce from such places,
so it's cheaper, but still delicious.

366
00:13:58,404 --> 00:14:01,607
-And you have your haul in here? Okay.
-Yes.

367
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:03,576
-Let's take a look.
-From the top.

368
00:14:03,642 --> 00:14:05,044
The top shelf.

369
00:14:05,110 --> 00:14:06,979
These are… Oh, eggs.

370
00:14:07,046 --> 00:14:10,749
-They're bluish.
-Those are blue-shelled eggs.

371
00:14:10,816 --> 00:14:12,318
-And they're tiny.
-That's cool.

372
00:14:12,384 --> 00:14:15,387
From Jecheon, my friend…

373
00:14:15,454 --> 00:14:17,356
-From our Jecheon.
-Yes, from Jecheon?

374
00:14:17,423 --> 00:14:19,458
Yes, my friend runs a restaurant,

375
00:14:19,525 --> 00:14:22,127
so I get those eggs
from the chicken farm that does business

376
00:14:22,194 --> 00:14:23,195
with my friend.

377
00:14:23,596 --> 00:14:25,531
WOW

378
00:14:25,598 --> 00:14:28,534
There are some sauces down here.

379
00:14:28,601 --> 00:14:30,870
I probably have lots of sauces.

380
00:14:30,936 --> 00:14:32,972
Yes, you have them here and here.

381
00:14:33,038 --> 00:14:35,341
-Those are all low-sugar sauces.
-They are.

382
00:14:35,407 --> 00:14:38,310
Yes, these are all labeled "low-sugar."
Low-sugar oyster sauce.

383
00:14:38,377 --> 00:14:40,713
This is low-sugar too.
A sponge cake, castella.

384
00:14:40,779 --> 00:14:43,916
I've kept a low-carb, high-fat diet
for a long time.

385
00:14:44,783 --> 00:14:46,018
Ever since I started,

386
00:14:46,085 --> 00:14:48,921
my hormone balance

387
00:14:48,988 --> 00:14:51,357
and the quality of sleep
improved drastically.

388
00:14:52,057 --> 00:14:53,325
After your low-carb, high-fat diet?

389
00:14:53,392 --> 00:14:57,196
She chugs bottles of every kind of spirit,
so how is her body age in her thirties?

390
00:14:57,263 --> 00:15:00,666
-That doesn't have an impact.
-It doesn't.

391
00:15:00,733 --> 00:15:03,936
I get that you have a low-carb diet,
but this is low-sugar.

392
00:15:04,003 --> 00:15:06,272
I've been doing low-carb, low-sugar.

393
00:15:06,338 --> 00:15:07,840
-Low-carb, low-sugar.
-Low-carb, low-sugar.

394
00:15:07,907 --> 00:15:09,975
-Do you not eat rice?
-I do.

395
00:15:10,042 --> 00:15:12,845
It's not that
I've cut out carbs altogether.

396
00:15:12,912 --> 00:15:15,881
-I keep it at 30%.
-It's low-carb. About 30%?

397
00:15:15,948 --> 00:15:17,616
-Yes, you don't cut it out.
-Right.

398
00:15:17,683 --> 00:15:20,352
What's this? Noodles?

399
00:15:20,419 --> 00:15:22,621
It's like tofu noodles.

400
00:15:22,688 --> 00:15:24,023
-Zero sugar.
-Yes.

401
00:15:24,089 --> 00:15:26,425
When I'm on a diet,
I always crave noodles.

402
00:15:26,492 --> 00:15:28,260
-So substitutes.
-Yes, substitutes.

403
00:15:28,327 --> 00:15:30,162
I substitute everything.

404
00:15:30,229 --> 00:15:32,031
The toughest craving is <i>jjajangmyeon.</i>

405
00:15:32,097 --> 00:15:33,565
Gosh, <i>jjajangmyeon.</i>

406
00:15:33,632 --> 00:15:35,668
-It's so good.
-Yes, it's so good.

407
00:15:35,734 --> 00:15:37,036
IT IS GOOD

408
00:15:37,102 --> 00:15:39,238
-Do you like strong-tasting food?
-Yes.

409
00:15:39,305 --> 00:15:40,806
I do, but I hold myself back.

410
00:15:40,873 --> 00:15:42,441
-She holds back.
-And she likes noodles.

411
00:15:42,508 --> 00:15:44,143
At times, when I get too hungry,

412
00:15:44,209 --> 00:15:45,144
I watch <i>mukbang.</i>

413
00:15:45,210 --> 00:15:48,447
LIVING VICARIOUSLY THROUGH MUKBANGS

414
00:15:48,514 --> 00:15:50,215
You can let loose once a month, right?

415
00:15:50,282 --> 00:15:52,351
Yes, so I eat it from time to time.

416
00:15:52,418 --> 00:15:54,119
After I eat something like that,

417
00:15:54,186 --> 00:15:56,722
I stay on an empty stomach
for 18 to 20 hours.

418
00:15:56,789 --> 00:15:57,756
You fast?

419
00:15:57,823 --> 00:16:00,793
People who try to keep in shape
usually fast.

420
00:16:01,493 --> 00:16:04,330
THE BEST AT SELF-DISCIPLINE

421
00:16:04,396 --> 00:16:06,298
I see some side dishes back here.

422
00:16:06,365 --> 00:16:08,100
-Yes.
-Side dishes.

423
00:16:08,167 --> 00:16:09,168
I made that one.

424
00:16:09,234 --> 00:16:10,235
A SIDE DISH SHE MADE?

425
00:16:10,302 --> 00:16:12,104
-You made it?
-What is it? Anchovies?

426
00:16:12,171 --> 00:16:14,606
-Stir-fried anchovies.
-With nuts.

427
00:16:14,673 --> 00:16:17,743
I had already added lots of allulose,

428
00:16:17,810 --> 00:16:20,612
so I regret adding
another dash at the end.

429
00:16:20,679 --> 00:16:22,781
She made it, so we should all try some.

430
00:16:22,848 --> 00:16:25,217
-You're trying it?
-We will.

431
00:16:26,051 --> 00:16:28,320
-I'm so nervous.
-It's really good.

432
00:16:28,387 --> 00:16:30,255
-It is? It is, right?
-Yes.

433
00:16:30,322 --> 00:16:32,791
It seems like she can cook.

434
00:16:32,858 --> 00:16:34,193
-Whoa.
-Hey.

435
00:16:34,259 --> 00:16:36,195
It's just sweet enough.

436
00:16:36,261 --> 00:16:37,596
I want some rice.

437
00:16:38,764 --> 00:16:41,033
-Just have a taste.
-You aren't eating a whole meal. Wait.

438
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:42,634
OBEDIENT…

439
00:16:42,701 --> 00:16:45,037
Is there anything else
you want to show off?

440
00:16:45,104 --> 00:16:46,538
My mom's salt-pickled cucumbers.

441
00:16:46,605 --> 00:16:48,941
-I love that.
-That sounds good.

442
00:16:49,008 --> 00:16:52,611
-It's perfect for summer.
-A summer delicacy.

443
00:16:52,678 --> 00:16:54,446
-It's great with rice.
-I should go again.

444
00:16:54,513 --> 00:16:55,381
-Again?
-I want to try it.

445
00:16:55,447 --> 00:16:57,116
He's trying that too?

446
00:16:57,649 --> 00:16:59,585
IT ALREADY LOOKS DELICIOUS

447
00:16:59,651 --> 00:17:03,088
-Mmm.
-Mmm.

448
00:17:03,155 --> 00:17:05,090
-It's not too salty.
-It's so good.

449
00:17:05,157 --> 00:17:07,092
-It's good.
-Isn't it seasoned perfectly?

450
00:17:07,159 --> 00:17:08,594
Whoa. This is to die for.

451
00:17:08,660 --> 00:17:09,962
It tastes like mom's cooking.

452
00:17:10,029 --> 00:17:11,130
It's really good.

453
00:17:11,196 --> 00:17:13,332
She'll be thrilled to see this.

454
00:17:13,399 --> 00:17:15,968
She loves this show.

455
00:17:16,535 --> 00:17:17,970
Mom, are you watching this?

456
00:17:18,504 --> 00:17:20,372
Ma'am, this is so good.

457
00:17:20,439 --> 00:17:23,475
-We'll call her Madam Pickles now.
-Yes.

458
00:17:23,542 --> 00:17:25,711
-Madam Pickles.
-Madam Pickles.

459
00:17:25,778 --> 00:17:27,479
-Madam Pickles.
-Yes.

460
00:17:27,546 --> 00:17:31,650
You have that DNA from your mother.

461
00:17:31,717 --> 00:17:34,253
And down here. Vegetables.

462
00:17:34,319 --> 00:17:35,154
She has lots.

463
00:17:35,220 --> 00:17:37,489
I see cucumbers and cabbage in here.

464
00:17:37,556 --> 00:17:39,158
You must like carrots.

465
00:17:39,224 --> 00:17:41,593
I love carrots so much.

466
00:17:41,660 --> 00:17:44,329
So when I'm at a supermarket
or when I shop for produce online,

467
00:17:44,396 --> 00:17:46,498
I buy them out of habit,
even when I have some.

468
00:17:46,565 --> 00:17:50,669
-Not many like these. Some pick them out.
-Right?

469
00:17:50,736 --> 00:17:54,139
I love the sweetness that comes
when carrots are cooked.

470
00:17:54,807 --> 00:17:55,641
You're right.

471
00:17:55,707 --> 00:17:59,011
Are these cucumbers? Yellow cucumbers?

472
00:17:59,078 --> 00:18:00,512
-I've never seen them.
-Zucchinis.

473
00:18:00,579 --> 00:18:02,781
-That's a zucchini.
-Zucchini?

474
00:18:02,848 --> 00:18:04,850
It's excellent for summer pasta.

475
00:18:04,917 --> 00:18:06,318
Whoa.

476
00:18:06,385 --> 00:18:07,486
-So it's a thing?
-What's it like?

477
00:18:07,553 --> 00:18:09,254
Don't you know, Chef Poong?

478
00:18:09,321 --> 00:18:12,424
I'm not a chef, so I don't really know.

479
00:18:12,491 --> 00:18:13,859
I don't think that's a popular ingredient.

480
00:18:13,926 --> 00:18:16,662
-We've never seen it on the show.
-Really?

481
00:18:16,728 --> 00:18:17,930
Where did you get these?

482
00:18:17,996 --> 00:18:20,232
-At the farmers' market.
-The farmers' market.

483
00:18:20,299 --> 00:18:24,002
-It's not easy to find them.
-So you got these there?

484
00:18:24,069 --> 00:18:26,071
What's this?

485
00:18:26,138 --> 00:18:28,841
-Those are really pretty.
-What's this? Broccoli?

486
00:18:28,907 --> 00:18:31,143
-Romanesco broccoli?
-Yes.

487
00:18:31,210 --> 00:18:33,512
-Yes.
-Romanesco broccoli?

488
00:18:33,579 --> 00:18:36,715
-A mix of cauliflower and broccoli.
-And broccoli.

489
00:18:36,782 --> 00:18:39,251
It's similar,
but that tastes much more buttery.

490
00:18:40,152 --> 00:18:41,987
-Lettuce.
-Lettuce.

491
00:18:42,054 --> 00:18:43,489
No, that isn't lettuce.

492
00:18:43,555 --> 00:18:45,958
-It's red <i>shiso.</i>
-Red lettuce?

493
00:18:46,024 --> 00:18:47,359
-<i>Shiso?</i>
-It's red <i>shiso?</i>

494
00:18:47,426 --> 00:18:48,994
<i>-Shiso.</i>
<i>-Shiso.</i> I thought it was lettuce.

495
00:18:49,061 --> 00:18:51,697
I thought it was lettuce, but it's not.

496
00:18:51,763 --> 00:18:53,565
I really love those.

497
00:18:53,632 --> 00:18:55,267
I eat them raw as a veggie wrap,

498
00:18:55,334 --> 00:18:57,069
and I add them to water too, right there.

499
00:18:57,136 --> 00:18:58,337
-This one?
-Whoa.

500
00:18:59,505 --> 00:19:01,607
-What's this?
-I see the red <i>shiso</i> here.

501
00:19:01,673 --> 00:19:04,076
-All of my favorites are in it.
-What's in here?

502
00:19:04,143 --> 00:19:05,811
Herbs, mint,

503
00:19:05,878 --> 00:19:08,747
basil, red <i>shiso,</i> cucumber,

504
00:19:08,814 --> 00:19:10,415
and lemon.

505
00:19:10,916 --> 00:19:11,984
REFRESHING VEGGIES, MUSTER

506
00:19:12,050 --> 00:19:14,553
-It's refreshing when you drink it.
-It is.

507
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:17,356
What's your favorite among these?

508
00:19:17,422 --> 00:19:18,957
Supersweet corn.

509
00:19:19,024 --> 00:19:21,393
-Supersweet corn?
-Supersweet corn.

510
00:19:21,460 --> 00:19:23,061
I can't hold back from eating those.

511
00:19:23,128 --> 00:19:25,063
-It's tough to hold back from.
-They're crunchy.

512
00:19:25,130 --> 00:19:27,633
I see some fruits,
like watermelon, tomatoes, and apples.

513
00:19:27,699 --> 00:19:30,502
Look how vibrant the color is.

514
00:19:30,569 --> 00:19:32,137
-That's the one I got…
-It's bright red.

515
00:19:32,204 --> 00:19:33,739
-What is that?
-…from the ugly produce market.

516
00:19:33,805 --> 00:19:36,275
It's from the ugly produce market?

517
00:19:36,341 --> 00:19:39,311
-Less conventionally valuable.
-With little flaws.

518
00:19:39,378 --> 00:19:42,514
But look at these.
They come in different sizes too.

519
00:19:42,581 --> 00:19:44,449
They're not that ugly.

520
00:19:44,516 --> 00:19:46,185
It's bright red.

521
00:19:46,251 --> 00:19:48,554
All of her vegetables look great.

522
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:50,522
She tries very hard to keep in shape,

523
00:19:50,589 --> 00:19:53,959
but she still keeps
these bottles next to everything.

524
00:19:54,026 --> 00:19:55,894
Is this vodka? What's this?

525
00:19:55,961 --> 00:19:58,330
It's probably some traditional liquor.

526
00:19:58,397 --> 00:19:59,798
-Traditional liquor?
-It was a present.

527
00:19:59,865 --> 00:20:02,534
-What's this? It's halfway gone.
-White wine.

528
00:20:02,601 --> 00:20:04,536
I had leftover wine, so I kept it

529
00:20:04,603 --> 00:20:05,804
for cooking.

530
00:20:05,871 --> 00:20:07,139
-For cooking?
-Yes.

531
00:20:07,206 --> 00:20:08,540
Most people say that.

532
00:20:09,975 --> 00:20:11,643
I usually pop a new bottle when I drink.

533
00:20:13,679 --> 00:20:16,215
You usually keep in shape,
but you drink a little, right?

534
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:17,883
Yes, to keep my feelings in shape.

535
00:20:17,950 --> 00:20:20,152
PRODUCE FOR HER BODY,
DRINKS FOR HER FEELINGS

536
00:20:20,219 --> 00:20:22,588
I have to keep
my feelings in shape every day.

537
00:20:22,955 --> 00:20:24,389
CAN BE HARMFUL WHEN OVERDONE

538
00:20:24,456 --> 00:20:25,757
He's serious about that.

539
00:20:25,824 --> 00:20:29,561
She's the master of self-discipline,
who takes care of her body and feelings.

540
00:20:29,628 --> 00:20:31,830
That's all for Jung-hwa's fridge!

541
00:20:32,331 --> 00:20:34,800
-You have a lot of stuff.
-I do, right?

542
00:20:34,866 --> 00:20:37,769
Currently, six chefs remain.

543
00:20:37,836 --> 00:20:39,871
-That's why…
-He's right.

544
00:20:39,938 --> 00:20:42,307
…one of the rounds
will be played as teams.

545
00:20:42,374 --> 00:20:43,775
-Wow.
-A team round.

546
00:20:43,842 --> 00:20:45,577
A two-on-two round.

547
00:20:45,644 --> 00:20:47,646
For this team round,

548
00:20:47,713 --> 00:20:50,682
the two chefs
aren't going to cook at once.

549
00:20:50,749 --> 00:20:53,252
-It'll be a tag team round.
-A tag team round.

550
00:20:53,318 --> 00:20:54,553
Taking turns.

551
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:56,755
-That sounds like so much fun. My gosh.
-It does.

552
00:20:56,822 --> 00:20:59,124
THAT'S TOTALLY YAREU

553
00:20:59,191 --> 00:21:02,494
-So two chefs will cook in relay.
-A tag team round.

554
00:21:02,561 --> 00:21:04,329
It's our first two-on-two tag team round.

555
00:21:04,396 --> 00:21:06,231
I'm so excited for this.

556
00:21:06,298 --> 00:21:08,400
There are people who always play in teams.

557
00:21:08,467 --> 00:21:11,403
-Some chefs are good at this.
-Like Ho-young and Sam.

558
00:21:11,470 --> 00:21:13,739
-Why don't you give it a go?
-Actually,

559
00:21:13,805 --> 00:21:15,874
we've always cooked individually,

560
00:21:15,941 --> 00:21:18,510
-but it felt like a team round.
-Right.

561
00:21:18,577 --> 00:21:20,545
SAM AND HO-YOUNG'S ONE-TEAM SPIRIT

562
00:21:20,612 --> 00:21:22,914
I've always played as a team,

563
00:21:22,981 --> 00:21:25,517
-so we probably have good teamwork.
-All right.

564
00:21:25,584 --> 00:21:26,852
All right, then.

565
00:21:26,918 --> 00:21:30,489
Does anybody want
to go against Sam and Ho-young?

566
00:21:30,555 --> 00:21:31,990
I'll go.

567
00:21:32,691 --> 00:21:34,192
-Who should I take?
-With whom?

568
00:21:34,259 --> 00:21:36,028
-You need a partner.
-You need someone.

569
00:21:36,094 --> 00:21:37,729
-I'll take…
-Someone has to want to join.

570
00:21:37,796 --> 00:21:40,565
…someone I deem my younger sister.

571
00:21:40,632 --> 00:21:45,070
I thought it'd be nice to cook
with Moon-jung, Chef Moon-jung today.

572
00:21:45,137 --> 00:21:46,772
So she'll do the heavy lifting.

573
00:21:46,838 --> 00:21:48,240
What do you think, Moon-jung?

574
00:21:48,307 --> 00:21:51,376
When my skills meet Poong's creativity,

575
00:21:51,443 --> 00:21:54,413
-it'll be a great collaboration.
-That sounds great.

576
00:21:54,479 --> 00:21:56,615
No matter what the order is,

577
00:21:56,682 --> 00:21:58,550
Sam and Ho-young

578
00:21:58,617 --> 00:22:01,753
-will go against Poong and Moon-jung.
-We'll fight.

579
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:03,455
That automatically leaves

580
00:22:03,522 --> 00:22:06,325
Jong-won and Yong-wook.

581
00:22:06,391 --> 00:22:09,294
You'll have to face each other
no matter what the order is.

582
00:22:09,361 --> 00:22:11,530
Now, let us show you

583
00:22:11,596 --> 00:22:13,632
the orders Jung-hwa brought.

584
00:22:13,699 --> 00:22:15,500
-The orders.
-What is it?

585
00:22:15,567 --> 00:22:17,202
-Okay, Land.
-Okay, Land.

586
00:22:17,269 --> 00:22:19,771
-Okay, Land?
-What is it?

587
00:22:19,838 --> 00:22:24,710
We spent a long time on a cruise ship
while filming <i>Okay! Madam 2.</i>

588
00:22:24,776 --> 00:22:28,880
So at times, we missed the food
we could have back on land.

589
00:22:28,947 --> 00:22:33,418
When you're on a ship,
you can be anywhere when you wake up.

590
00:22:33,485 --> 00:22:35,954
So I want you to get off
wherever you would like

591
00:22:36,021 --> 00:22:37,689
and make me something delicious.

592
00:22:39,057 --> 00:22:40,325
The country doesn't matter.

593
00:22:40,392 --> 00:22:42,227
-No.
-It's not designated.

594
00:22:42,294 --> 00:22:45,197
You're free to choose
whichever country you wish to go to.

595
00:22:45,263 --> 00:22:47,499
-We can go to China.
-But a dish from the land.

596
00:22:47,566 --> 00:22:49,434
-A dish from the land.
-China.

597
00:22:49,501 --> 00:22:51,670
The next one. What's the next order?

598
00:22:51,737 --> 00:22:53,572
What's Jung-hwa looking to eat?

599
00:22:53,638 --> 00:22:55,407
-Junky Low-Carb, Low-Sugar.
-Junky Low-Carb, Low-Sugar

600
00:22:55,474 --> 00:22:58,143
There are times
when I crave that junk food flavor,

601
00:22:58,210 --> 00:23:01,213
and I sometimes fall asleep
while watching <i>mukbang,</i>

602
00:23:01,279 --> 00:23:03,849
which makes my mouth water so much.

603
00:23:03,915 --> 00:23:05,817
Something that has
minimal carbs and sugar,

604
00:23:05,884 --> 00:23:07,386
yet tastes junky, and deadass deli…

605
00:23:07,452 --> 00:23:08,820
OOPS…

606
00:23:08,887 --> 00:23:11,223
-Deadass delicious.
-Junky yet very delicious.

607
00:23:11,289 --> 00:23:12,591
Something that good.

608
00:23:12,657 --> 00:23:15,927
So low-carb, low-sugar, deadass delicious.

609
00:23:17,662 --> 00:23:19,464
Feel free to fixate on "junky" flavor.

610
00:23:19,531 --> 00:23:21,299
-Something junky? Okay.
-Yes.

611
00:23:21,366 --> 00:23:25,103
Director Yong-wook,
you're still considered a rookie here.

612
00:23:25,170 --> 00:23:27,105
He hasn't been here for long.

613
00:23:27,172 --> 00:23:28,407
You'll get the first pick.

614
00:23:28,473 --> 00:23:30,642
-I'm so excited.
-The food I make

615
00:23:30,709 --> 00:23:33,979
is something you'd say it's of the land,

616
00:23:34,045 --> 00:23:35,547
so I'll go with Okay, Land.

617
00:23:35,614 --> 00:23:37,115
Okay, Land.

618
00:23:37,349 --> 00:23:39,050
JONG-WON AUTOMATICALLY JOINS THE ROUND

619
00:23:39,117 --> 00:23:41,019
-Wait a second.
-Everything's decided.

620
00:23:41,086 --> 00:23:45,056
Then the team round will be
Junky Low-Carb, Low-Sugar.

621
00:23:45,123 --> 00:23:47,125
Can that team make junk food…

622
00:23:47,192 --> 00:23:48,794
I'm that junky guy.

623
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:50,729
Is that okay, Sam?

624
00:23:50,796 --> 00:23:52,864
-It sounds fun.
-Really?

625
00:23:52,931 --> 00:23:54,666
-Good.
-Then it's decided.

626
00:23:54,733 --> 00:23:57,869
Now, let us begin with the first round,
the individual match.

627
00:23:57,936 --> 00:24:02,240
Yong-wook and Jong-won's
15-minute cook-off is coming right up!

628
00:24:05,844 --> 00:24:10,115
<i>The first round of cook-off</i>
<i>with Jung-hwa's ingredients.</i>

629
00:24:10,182 --> 00:24:11,983
Now on the Road to Son,

630
00:24:12,050 --> 00:24:13,952
Director Yoo Yong-wook,

631
00:24:14,019 --> 00:24:15,654
versus the new gatekeeper,

632
00:24:15,720 --> 00:24:18,056
Chef Son Jong-won.

633
00:24:18,423 --> 00:24:20,058
EXCITED

634
00:24:20,125 --> 00:24:21,660
You've already defeated Poong.

635
00:24:22,093 --> 00:24:23,895
YOO YONG-WOOK DEFEATED POONG
IN THE DEBUT ROUND

636
00:24:23,962 --> 00:24:26,364
His next target is Jong-won.

637
00:24:26,431 --> 00:24:28,066
NEXT UP, THE CURRENT SOLE 1ST PLACE

638
00:24:28,133 --> 00:24:30,202
-Yong-wook said something like this.
-What?

639
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:35,240
"I won the previous cook-off

640
00:24:35,307 --> 00:24:37,275
thanks to Chef Jong-won."

641
00:24:37,342 --> 00:24:38,210
What do you mean?

642
00:24:38,577 --> 00:24:40,412
WHAT'S GOING ON BETWEEN YOU TWO?

643
00:24:40,479 --> 00:24:44,483
While I was literally shuddering
in the green room,

644
00:24:44,549 --> 00:24:47,152
someone suddenly came and patted my back.

645
00:24:47,219 --> 00:24:51,256
And it felt like,
"It's all right. You got this."

646
00:24:51,323 --> 00:24:54,826
I turned my head and saw my older bro
tapping my shoulder lightly.

647
00:24:55,727 --> 00:24:56,928
He's older?

648
00:24:56,995 --> 00:24:59,231
-I was born in 1986.
-We're 2 years apart.

649
00:24:59,297 --> 00:25:00,565
Two years?

650
00:25:00,632 --> 00:25:02,667
His help played a big role.

651
00:25:02,734 --> 00:25:04,669
Jong-won's now like a cult leader.

652
00:25:04,736 --> 00:25:06,671
He tapped Yong-wook's back,

653
00:25:06,738 --> 00:25:09,574
and he could suddenly see again.

654
00:25:09,641 --> 00:25:11,209
But I'm dead serious.

655
00:25:11,276 --> 00:25:15,614
My palpitating heart
suddenly calmed down instantly.

656
00:25:15,914 --> 00:25:17,782
PREACH IT

657
00:25:17,849 --> 00:25:19,818
I was parched,
and I had to get some water,

658
00:25:19,885 --> 00:25:21,553
so I was telling him to move.

659
00:25:21,620 --> 00:25:22,521
To move him?

660
00:25:22,587 --> 00:25:24,189
"Move it! I'm thirsty!"

661
00:25:24,256 --> 00:25:26,191
"Don't stand there."

662
00:25:26,258 --> 00:25:29,394
I don't like violence,
so I just tapped him gently.

663
00:25:29,461 --> 00:25:30,962
-So that was it.
-Like, "Excuse me."

664
00:25:31,029 --> 00:25:33,231
-"Excuse me"?
-"Excuse me"?

665
00:25:33,298 --> 00:25:35,500
-He went with it.
-He's a funny guy.

666
00:25:35,567 --> 00:25:37,536
You've been funny lately.

667
00:25:37,903 --> 00:25:39,638
MANAGED TO MAKE THE TOUGH JUNG-HWAN LAUGH

668
00:25:39,704 --> 00:25:42,207
You're funny. Jong-won's funny.

669
00:25:42,274 --> 00:25:43,775
AMAZED

670
00:25:43,842 --> 00:25:46,711
-He's now funny too.
-He needs someone to get it out of him.

671
00:25:46,778 --> 00:25:47,812
EXCUSE ME, COMEDIANS

672
00:25:47,879 --> 00:25:50,882
-They have something in common.
-They do?

673
00:25:50,949 --> 00:25:52,417
-They have something in common.
-Yes.

674
00:25:52,484 --> 00:25:53,718
They have both

675
00:25:53,785 --> 00:25:56,721
been in charge
of the Presidential state dinner.

676
00:25:56,788 --> 00:25:57,856
-Really?
-Is that so?

677
00:25:57,923 --> 00:25:59,691
Do you remember that day, Jong-won?

678
00:25:59,758 --> 00:26:02,427
I remember President Macron telling me

679
00:26:02,494 --> 00:26:05,130
that our team had great teamwork,
and he said, "Bravo."

680
00:26:05,196 --> 00:26:07,699
He even said, "Bravo, Chef."

681
00:26:07,866 --> 00:26:09,167
FULLY CHARGED WITH EXCITEMENT

682
00:26:10,001 --> 00:26:12,070
DIRECTOR YONG-WOOK'S INGREDIENTS:
CARROT, CABBAGE, ZUCCHINI, RED ONION

683
00:26:12,137 --> 00:26:14,172
MINI BELL PEPPER, CUCUMBER, TOMATO,
SALSA VERDE, RED SHISO, AND MORE

684
00:26:14,239 --> 00:26:16,274
Director Yong-wook, what are you making?

685
00:26:16,341 --> 00:26:18,143
My dish is called

686
00:26:18,209 --> 00:26:20,111
Brazil, <i>Yareu.</i>

687
00:26:20,178 --> 00:26:21,880
-Brazil, <i>Yareu.</i>
-Brazil, <i>Yareu.</i>

688
00:26:21,947 --> 00:26:24,049
-Brazil, <i>Yareu.</i>
-Brazil, <i>Yareu.</i>

689
00:26:24,115 --> 00:26:25,750
-Brazil, <i>Yareu.</i>
-Brazil, <i>Yareu.</i>

690
00:26:25,817 --> 00:26:28,353
-It sounds somewhat passionate.
-It does.

691
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:32,257
When I imagined a cruise docked at a port,

692
00:26:32,324 --> 00:26:35,226
it reminded me of her explosive energy.

693
00:26:35,293 --> 00:26:38,196
I wondered which country has
that kind of energy

694
00:26:38,263 --> 00:26:40,899
-and decided to dock in Brazil.
-Brazil's great.

695
00:26:40,966 --> 00:26:43,902
Now, there's a deep-fried Brazilian dish
called <i>pastel</i>.

696
00:26:43,969 --> 00:26:45,136
Yes, <i>pastel</i>.

697
00:26:45,203 --> 00:26:46,972
It's made with

698
00:26:47,038 --> 00:26:51,343
a hearty amount of filling
inside a wrapper, then deep-fried.

699
00:26:51,409 --> 00:26:53,345
So I'll make that.

700
00:26:53,411 --> 00:26:56,114
You said you love supersweet corn,

701
00:26:56,181 --> 00:26:58,283
so I'll add
a bit of a South American touch

702
00:26:58,350 --> 00:27:00,318
-by grilling them over fire.
-Sounds great.

703
00:27:00,385 --> 00:27:02,320
I'll also make a cocktail
called Caipirinha.

704
00:27:02,387 --> 00:27:03,722
-Caipirinha.
-Yes.

705
00:27:03,788 --> 00:27:06,191
It's a Brazilian cocktail made with lime.

706
00:27:06,257 --> 00:27:08,927
You don't have the actual liquor,
but I can make it taste similar,

707
00:27:08,994 --> 00:27:10,729
so I'll use the liquor you had

708
00:27:10,795 --> 00:27:14,165
and make you a nice meal
alongside a Caipirinha.

709
00:27:14,466 --> 00:27:17,102
ALMOST TOO MUCH YAREU

710
00:27:17,936 --> 00:27:19,971
CHEF JONG-WON'S INGREDIENTS:
ZUCCHINI, MINI WATERMELON, RED ONION

711
00:27:20,038 --> 00:27:22,107
SUPERSWEET CORN, ROMANESCO BROCCOLI,
RED SHISO, TOMATO PASTE, AND MORE

712
00:27:22,173 --> 00:27:25,477
Jong-won is up next. What are you making?

713
00:27:25,543 --> 00:27:27,345
I want to take Jung-hwa

714
00:27:27,412 --> 00:27:30,148
-on a world tour.
-A world tour?

715
00:27:30,215 --> 00:27:31,616
Where are you starting?

716
00:27:31,683 --> 00:27:33,184
The name of my dish is

717
00:27:33,251 --> 00:27:36,087
South America, Europe, Korea,

718
00:27:36,154 --> 00:27:37,956
<i>Yaho.</i>

719
00:27:38,023 --> 00:27:39,524
<i>Yaho.</i>

720
00:27:39,991 --> 00:27:42,127
THE CRUISE PASSENGER LOOKS PLEASED

721
00:27:42,327 --> 00:27:43,828
AS ALWAYS, TRIED TOO HARD…

722
00:27:44,029 --> 00:27:45,163
CLAPPING…

723
00:27:45,697 --> 00:27:48,667
CHEF & MY FRIDGE ALSO HAS WONI,
SON JONG-WONI

724
00:27:48,733 --> 00:27:51,936
-<i>Yaho.</i> My gosh.
-Smile. Smile a little.

725
00:27:52,003 --> 00:27:54,205
-That was almost too funny.
-Wasn't it?

726
00:27:54,272 --> 00:27:55,407
So just now,

727
00:27:55,473 --> 00:27:58,443
my toes even curled. Can't you see?

728
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:00,512
-Like this?
-Yes, they curled.

729
00:28:00,578 --> 00:28:02,447
-I know what that is.
-It was too funny.

730
00:28:02,514 --> 00:28:05,316
If you saw him at his restaurant,
you'd never think he'd be like this.

731
00:28:05,383 --> 00:28:07,185
-My gosh.
-He only does it for the show.

732
00:28:07,252 --> 00:28:08,853
-Really.
-For laughs.

733
00:28:08,920 --> 00:28:10,555
So what are you making?

734
00:28:10,622 --> 00:28:13,191
I believe vegetable dishes
are truly the food of the land,

735
00:28:13,258 --> 00:28:15,293
because they can only grow on land.

736
00:28:15,360 --> 00:28:17,529
-So today, I will…
-What's he doing?

737
00:28:18,596 --> 00:28:20,198
LEAVING THE KITCHEN WITH A VEGGIE BASKET

738
00:28:20,765 --> 00:28:22,000
That's so pretty.

739
00:28:22,067 --> 00:28:23,835
My gosh. What is that?

740
00:28:23,902 --> 00:28:26,371
BRINGS THE VEGGIES TO JUNG-HWA!

741
00:28:26,438 --> 00:28:30,709
I prepared three different dishes today.
And they are…

742
00:28:30,775 --> 00:28:32,877
EMERGENCY

743
00:28:32,944 --> 00:28:34,713
It's a fine dining experience.

744
00:28:34,779 --> 00:28:35,880
First off,

745
00:28:35,947 --> 00:28:39,651
I'll use watermelon and red <i>shiso</i>

746
00:28:39,718 --> 00:28:43,154
-to make South America's ceviche.
-It already sounds good.

747
00:28:43,221 --> 00:28:47,592
Next, I'll use the golden zucchini
and make zucchini steak.

748
00:28:47,659 --> 00:28:49,561
Lastly, I'll use supersweet corn and make

749
00:28:49,627 --> 00:28:51,996
-corn <i>beomuri</i>, steamed rice cake.
-Corn <i>beomuri?</i>

750
00:28:52,063 --> 00:28:55,233
That way, you'll experience
that warm welcome back home.

751
00:28:55,300 --> 00:28:56,334
There's a story to it.

752
00:28:56,401 --> 00:28:58,269
-Would you like to take a picture?
-Sure.

753
00:28:58,336 --> 00:29:01,172
They look different like that,
unlike how they were in the fridge.

754
00:29:01,239 --> 00:29:02,240
A fine dining experience.

755
00:29:02,307 --> 00:29:03,308
CLICK

756
00:29:04,843 --> 00:29:06,845
#FINE_DINING_YAREU

757
00:29:06,911 --> 00:29:09,147
-Shall we go on this cruise trip?
-Sure thing.

758
00:29:09,214 --> 00:29:11,449
Where should the ship dock?

759
00:29:11,516 --> 00:29:16,054
Let us begin the cook-off
titled Okay, Land for Jung-hwa.

760
00:29:16,121 --> 00:29:19,958
The global unorthodox chef
approved by the Brazilian president,

761
00:29:20,024 --> 00:29:22,894
-Samba Yoo.
-Samba?

762
00:29:22,961 --> 00:29:24,195
Samba Yoo.

763
00:29:24,262 --> 00:29:26,564
The French president
left him a comment, "Bravo, Chef."

764
00:29:27,132 --> 00:29:31,069
Bonjour Son, Chef Son Jong-won
is up for the challenge.

765
00:29:31,136 --> 00:29:34,005
To which chef will Jung-hwa say okay to?

766
00:29:34,072 --> 00:29:37,509
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

767
00:29:37,575 --> 00:29:38,710
Let's go!

768
00:29:39,911 --> 00:29:41,746
THE CRUISE TAKES OFF

769
00:29:44,149 --> 00:29:45,750
You're making the wrapper now?

770
00:29:45,817 --> 00:29:47,652
I'm looking forward
to the Brazilian dishes.

771
00:29:47,719 --> 00:29:50,321
-Is that starch?
-Right? What's that? Starch?

772
00:29:50,388 --> 00:29:52,791
-It's starch and flour.
-Flour and starch.

773
00:29:52,857 --> 00:29:55,126
-Olive oil?
-Yes, I'll deep-fry this later.

774
00:29:55,193 --> 00:29:56,961
Are you making the <i>pastel</i>?

775
00:29:57,028 --> 00:29:58,129
-You're right.
-Salt.

776
00:29:58,196 --> 00:29:59,731
That's a unique way of making dough.

777
00:29:59,798 --> 00:30:02,934
-Director Yong-wook is at it.
-He could've just used

778
00:30:03,001 --> 00:30:04,502
the immersion blender.

779
00:30:04,569 --> 00:30:06,971
-It's okay. It only takes two minutes.
-Really?

780
00:30:07,038 --> 00:30:09,007
Then I'll let it rest.

781
00:30:09,073 --> 00:30:10,909
We usually stick to the immersion blender.

782
00:30:10,975 --> 00:30:12,243
But he doesn't trust it yet.

783
00:30:12,310 --> 00:30:14,479
I haven't gotten used to it.
I don't know what it's like.

784
00:30:14,546 --> 00:30:17,315
-So he's using his hands.
-He'd rather do it himself.

785
00:30:17,382 --> 00:30:19,284
-But it takes more time.
-It seems so.

786
00:30:19,350 --> 00:30:21,152
I have enough time.

787
00:30:21,219 --> 00:30:23,288
-Are those breadcrumbs? Rice.
-What's that?

788
00:30:23,354 --> 00:30:25,089
-Rice?
-Rice.

789
00:30:25,156 --> 00:30:27,826
That's how it turned out?

790
00:30:27,892 --> 00:30:29,227
-Nice job.
-What's that?

791
00:30:29,294 --> 00:30:32,831
-Rice powder? That's how you do it?
-He made it himself.

792
00:30:32,897 --> 00:30:35,733
I'm using rice to make <i>beomuri.</i>

793
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:39,838
I'll use her supersweet corn
that a farmer worked very hard on.

794
00:30:39,904 --> 00:30:42,173
-The supersweet corn.
-Corn.

795
00:30:42,240 --> 00:30:43,308
GRINDING

796
00:30:43,374 --> 00:30:44,576
He's blending the corn.

797
00:30:44,642 --> 00:30:46,711
-That must be good as is.
-I agree.

798
00:30:46,778 --> 00:30:49,848
This means Chef Jong-won
has saved quite a lot of time.

799
00:30:49,914 --> 00:30:52,283
The dumpling wrapper.

800
00:30:52,350 --> 00:30:54,919
Not only must the wrapper turn out nicely,
but once he fries it,

801
00:30:54,986 --> 00:30:56,921
it'll take a while
for the filling to cook.

802
00:30:56,988 --> 00:30:58,456
-I see.
-Done.

803
00:30:58,523 --> 00:31:00,792
-All right.
-He's resting it.

804
00:31:00,859 --> 00:31:02,193
You're going to rest it too?

805
00:31:02,260 --> 00:31:03,294
Five minutes.

806
00:31:03,361 --> 00:31:06,564
-Five minutes?
-You'll let it rest for five minutes?

807
00:31:06,631 --> 00:31:09,100
I'll let him know at eight minutes.

808
00:31:09,167 --> 00:31:12,403
The rice? What's he doing
with the rice powder? <i>Baekseolgi?</i>

809
00:31:12,470 --> 00:31:13,671
<i>Beomuri.</i>

810
00:31:13,738 --> 00:31:16,307
MAKING BEOMURI

811
00:31:16,374 --> 00:31:18,743
-It's similar to <i>seolgi.</i>
-You'll steam it?

812
00:31:18,810 --> 00:31:22,947
The dough is usually made with water,
but I'll use corn juice

813
00:31:23,014 --> 00:31:24,782
to add a nice color.

814
00:31:24,849 --> 00:31:27,252
He's using lots of corn.

815
00:31:27,952 --> 00:31:28,920
How pretty.

816
00:31:28,987 --> 00:31:29,988
MIX WELL, POUR INTO A STEAMER

817
00:31:31,823 --> 00:31:33,157
-Is that…
-He's spreading it even.

818
00:31:33,224 --> 00:31:36,027
Isn't it too thin for <i>seolgi?</i>

819
00:31:36,094 --> 00:31:37,729
To let it cook quickly.

820
00:31:37,795 --> 00:31:39,864
-It's <i>beomuri.</i>
-I'm so excited.

821
00:31:39,931 --> 00:31:41,299
Cabbage.

822
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:43,034
MAKING THE DUMPLING FILLING

823
00:31:43,101 --> 00:31:44,669
He's julienning the cabbage.

824
00:31:44,736 --> 00:31:46,604
2. THINLY SLICE THE CABBAGE

825
00:31:46,671 --> 00:31:49,741
Yong-wook is using
both carrots and zucchinis.

826
00:31:49,807 --> 00:31:53,511
Jung-hwa especially
loves carrots very much.

827
00:31:53,578 --> 00:31:54,779
She juices it every morning.

828
00:31:55,346 --> 00:31:58,182
Is he grilling the cabbage?

829
00:31:58,249 --> 00:31:59,284
On a gridiron?

830
00:31:59,350 --> 00:32:00,985
-This is his thing.
-Cabbage.

831
00:32:01,052 --> 00:32:03,087
-Zucchini.
-Grilling is his thing.

832
00:32:03,721 --> 00:32:07,425
All those ingredients
are something that I love, so…

833
00:32:07,492 --> 00:32:09,227
-You must be excited.
-Yes.

834
00:32:09,294 --> 00:32:10,328
Next, hanging tender.

835
00:32:10,395 --> 00:32:12,497
-Good.
-He's grilling meat,

836
00:32:12,563 --> 00:32:15,300
-…although he didn't bring his grill.
-Hanging tender.

837
00:32:15,366 --> 00:32:18,102
He's using four… no, three burners
to grill everything.

838
00:32:18,169 --> 00:32:21,439
I'm making the first vegetable dish,
vegetarian dish of the show.

839
00:32:21,506 --> 00:32:23,041
-It's vegetarian?
-Vegetarian?

840
00:32:23,107 --> 00:32:25,243
-It's vegetarian.
-Only vegetables?

841
00:32:25,310 --> 00:32:26,878
He grabs the zucchinis.

842
00:32:26,945 --> 00:32:28,846
That's what it looks like in the middle.

843
00:32:29,213 --> 00:32:30,315
TAPPING

844
00:32:30,381 --> 00:32:31,215
What's he doing?

845
00:32:32,717 --> 00:32:35,186
-He's making cuts.
-He's making cuts?

846
00:32:35,253 --> 00:32:36,888
-But why?
-To make them pretty.

847
00:32:36,955 --> 00:32:39,123
-It's to let them cook well.
-To let them cook well.

848
00:32:39,958 --> 00:32:42,527
INTO A HEATED PAN

849
00:32:42,593 --> 00:32:44,295
You're using no meat, Jong-won?

850
00:32:44,963 --> 00:32:46,664
-Sorry? No meat.
-No meat?

851
00:32:46,731 --> 00:32:49,067
-No meat.
-How fun.

852
00:32:49,267 --> 00:32:50,268
FUN POINTS, 5

853
00:32:50,335 --> 00:32:53,371
-This is fun.
-He grabbed that new veggie.

854
00:32:53,438 --> 00:32:54,672
Romanesco broccoli.

855
00:32:54,739 --> 00:32:56,474
It's a type of cauliflower.

856
00:32:56,541 --> 00:32:58,710
-It looks like broccoli.
-Let me cut some tiny pieces.

857
00:32:58,776 --> 00:33:00,678
It's all about delicate prepping.

858
00:33:00,945 --> 00:33:03,047
2. BLANCH ROMANESCO BROCCOLI
IN SALTED WATER, THEN COOL

859
00:33:03,281 --> 00:33:05,483
NEXT UP, RED SHISO AND MINT!

860
00:33:06,150 --> 00:33:08,753
CHOPPING WITH GOOD VIBES

861
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:10,855
That's so cool.

862
00:33:10,922 --> 00:33:12,557
That's really cool.

863
00:33:13,791 --> 00:33:15,526
RED ONION
PEPPER

864
00:33:15,827 --> 00:33:17,528
DICE!

865
00:33:17,595 --> 00:33:18,896
It's red <i>shiso.</i>

866
00:33:19,230 --> 00:33:20,531
OLIVE OIL, LIME JUICE

867
00:33:20,598 --> 00:33:22,066
CEVICHE SAUCE IS DONE

868
00:33:22,133 --> 00:33:23,534
Ten minutes.

869
00:33:23,601 --> 00:33:25,003
You have ten minutes!

870
00:33:25,069 --> 00:33:26,971
-Ten minutes?
-Ten minutes.

871
00:33:27,038 --> 00:33:29,374
-What's he doing? A cucumber?
-Cucumber.

872
00:33:29,440 --> 00:33:30,608
Cucumber?

873
00:33:30,675 --> 00:33:31,809
Cucumber. For the sauce.

874
00:33:31,876 --> 00:33:33,778
-For the sauce.
-The sauce.

875
00:33:33,845 --> 00:33:35,680
Did I bite off more than I could chew?

876
00:33:36,080 --> 00:33:37,081
STARTING TO GET SCARED…

877
00:33:37,148 --> 00:33:39,050
It must be tough to follow through.

878
00:33:39,117 --> 00:33:40,618
Ten minutes.

879
00:33:40,685 --> 00:33:41,652
He's grating tomatoes too.

880
00:33:41,719 --> 00:33:43,755
-He's making salsa.
-Salsa.

881
00:33:43,821 --> 00:33:46,290
-What's that? Sun-dried tomato?
-Sun-dried tomato.

882
00:33:46,357 --> 00:33:47,592
Sun-dried tomato.

883
00:33:48,559 --> 00:33:50,228
Flip the meat once.

884
00:33:50,294 --> 00:33:52,997
-The meat.
-It's fine.

885
00:33:53,064 --> 00:33:54,065
It's still a while.

886
00:33:54,132 --> 00:33:58,469
Director Yong-wook surely does know
a lot about grilling food.

887
00:33:58,536 --> 00:34:00,505
-He can grill anything.
-It's hot.

888
00:34:00,571 --> 00:34:03,074
The veggies are a bit too big
to be used as the filling.

889
00:34:03,141 --> 00:34:05,343
I'm going to ch-ch-chop everything.

890
00:34:05,610 --> 00:34:07,478
STILL NOT AN EXPERT AT ANSWERING…

891
00:34:07,545 --> 00:34:09,313
Do-do-don't talk to him.

892
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:11,416
This is insane. I can hear stuff today.

893
00:34:11,482 --> 00:34:13,017
Last time, I couldn't hear anything.

894
00:34:13,084 --> 00:34:14,452
-This is bad.
-He can hear us today.

895
00:34:14,519 --> 00:34:16,087
You have to flip the meat!

896
00:34:16,154 --> 00:34:16,988
-The wrapper.
-It's fine.

897
00:34:17,054 --> 00:34:19,057
-It's still fine.
-It is?

898
00:34:19,123 --> 00:34:21,125
You said you'd rest the dough
only for five minutes!

899
00:34:21,192 --> 00:34:23,828
-No.
-Then get to it already.

900
00:34:23,895 --> 00:34:25,830
-No? Do you need more time?
-I'll get to it.

901
00:34:25,897 --> 00:34:27,732
Supersweet corn.

902
00:34:27,799 --> 00:34:30,134
If you're busy,
you don't have to answer us.

903
00:34:30,201 --> 00:34:32,003
-But I can hear you.
-You have to answer.

904
00:34:32,070 --> 00:34:33,704
Only then will you be on TV!

905
00:34:33,771 --> 00:34:35,673
Only your answers will make it on air!

906
00:34:35,740 --> 00:34:38,009
Poong, that's a warning.

907
00:34:38,076 --> 00:34:39,877
Yes, you were too loud.

908
00:34:41,145 --> 00:34:43,047
This is bad.

909
00:34:43,114 --> 00:34:45,917
-It's so hectic.
-Cooking wine.

910
00:34:45,983 --> 00:34:47,685
4. MIX CORN WITH VARIOUS CONDIMENTS

911
00:34:47,752 --> 00:34:49,787
He's using lots of corn.

912
00:34:51,122 --> 00:34:52,857
CHECKING ON THE ZUCCHINI

913
00:34:53,091 --> 00:34:54,425
COOKED TO GOLDEN BROWN

914
00:34:54,492 --> 00:34:56,294
The golden zucchini looks so good.

915
00:34:56,360 --> 00:34:58,463
He'll now start to <i>arroser</i>, or baste.

916
00:34:58,830 --> 00:35:02,033
ARROSER, BASTING:
COOKING BY POURING BUTTER OR OIL

917
00:35:02,100 --> 00:35:03,367
That looks good.

918
00:35:03,434 --> 00:35:04,669
How sexy.

919
00:35:04,735 --> 00:35:06,170
That's so sexy.

920
00:35:06,237 --> 00:35:08,072
People are cheering for him.

921
00:35:08,139 --> 00:35:09,707
My gosh!

922
00:35:09,774 --> 00:35:11,042
-He's so cool!
-My gosh!

923
00:35:11,309 --> 00:35:12,910
THIS ZUCCHINI IS TOTALLY…

924
00:35:12,977 --> 00:35:14,045
<i>-Yareu!</i>
<i>-Yareu!</i>

925
00:35:14,112 --> 00:35:15,446
<i>Yareu!</i>

926
00:35:15,513 --> 00:35:16,347
<i>Yareu!</i>

927
00:35:16,414 --> 00:35:17,415
-<i>Yareu!</i>
<i>-Yareu!</i>

928
00:35:17,482 --> 00:35:18,316
<i>Yareu!</i>

929
00:35:18,382 --> 00:35:20,017
-<i>Yareu!</i>
-Jong-won, <i>yaho.</i>

930
00:35:21,085 --> 00:35:23,254
WE'LL OVERUSE THIS MEME
AND PULL THE SHUTTERS DOWN

931
00:35:23,321 --> 00:35:25,790
-We can't use that anymore.
-<i>Yareu!</i>

932
00:35:26,157 --> 00:35:27,191
ZUCCHINI INTO THE OVEN!

933
00:35:27,258 --> 00:35:28,960
He's capturing the ladies' hearts.

934
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:32,130
Jong-won cuts the watermelon.

935
00:35:32,196 --> 00:35:33,464
He's making thin slices.

936
00:35:33,531 --> 00:35:34,632
Watermelon on a slicer?

937
00:35:35,533 --> 00:35:36,634
It's watermelon ceviche.

938
00:35:36,701 --> 00:35:38,703
-Watermelon ceviche?
-That sounds good.

939
00:35:38,769 --> 00:35:42,039
4. TOP WATERMELON WITH GROUND PEPPER,
OLIVE OIL, SALT, AND LIME ZEST

940
00:35:42,106 --> 00:35:43,341
That looks so good.

941
00:35:43,407 --> 00:35:44,842
Eight minutes.

942
00:35:44,909 --> 00:35:45,810
Okay.

943
00:35:45,877 --> 00:35:47,478
I love grilled veggies.

944
00:35:47,545 --> 00:35:48,746
GRABS THE VEGGIES FIRST

945
00:35:48,813 --> 00:35:50,481
-That looks so good.
-Right now…

946
00:35:50,548 --> 00:35:52,550
He has to make a Caipirinha too.

947
00:35:52,617 --> 00:35:54,318
-The dough.
-It takes a while to make one.

948
00:35:54,385 --> 00:35:56,387
-You're halfway through.
-I'm fine.

949
00:35:56,454 --> 00:35:58,956
An onion. A red onion.

950
00:35:59,157 --> 00:36:00,291
CHOPPING

951
00:36:01,092 --> 00:36:02,593
He's good with the knife.

952
00:36:02,660 --> 00:36:05,029
You two are both unorthodox,
yet the knife skills…

953
00:36:05,096 --> 00:36:06,464
-That's nice.
-They're different.

954
00:36:06,531 --> 00:36:08,866
-He's much better.
-He's much better than you.

955
00:36:08,933 --> 00:36:11,235
-You have no time.
-Chop the veggies.

956
00:36:11,302 --> 00:36:12,236
The dough.

957
00:36:12,303 --> 00:36:15,239
-He should make the wrapper.
-The dough?

958
00:36:15,306 --> 00:36:16,474
Can I ask Sung-jun for help?

959
00:36:16,541 --> 00:36:18,242
Can I ask Sung-jun for help?

960
00:36:18,309 --> 00:36:20,211
-I have to help?
-What now?

961
00:36:20,278 --> 00:36:21,612
-Suddenly?
-Suddenly?

962
00:36:21,679 --> 00:36:23,648
PICKED OUT OF THE BLUE

963
00:36:23,714 --> 00:36:25,116
PRETTY FAR DISTANCE

964
00:36:25,183 --> 00:36:27,118
I can't do that.

965
00:36:27,185 --> 00:36:29,153
That's not how you do it.

966
00:36:29,220 --> 00:36:30,788
It's not even allowed for Poong.

967
00:36:30,855 --> 00:36:32,290
Hurry up and do it.

968
00:36:33,824 --> 00:36:36,761
-The meat is ready.
-Yes, the meat is ready.

969
00:36:36,827 --> 00:36:38,129
He has no time. What will he do?

970
00:36:38,696 --> 00:36:40,431
-He really doesn't have time.
-Make just one.

971
00:36:40,498 --> 00:36:44,202
-Just use a little bit of dough.
-Make just one piece.

972
00:36:44,268 --> 00:36:46,637
-Not too much.
-That's how he's flattening it.

973
00:36:46,704 --> 00:36:48,439
-It's not working.
-It's not working.

974
00:36:49,240 --> 00:36:51,309
-This isn't working.
-It's not working.

975
00:36:51,375 --> 00:36:52,810
-Is there a rolling pin?
-Use your hands.

976
00:36:52,877 --> 00:36:54,845
-Use your hands.
-A pin would make it too thick.

977
00:36:54,912 --> 00:36:58,282
-Just let it go through a few times.
-Use your hands for now.

978
00:36:58,349 --> 00:37:01,052
-He tries the machine again.
-It's working.

979
00:37:01,118 --> 00:37:01,986
It's way too small.

980
00:37:02,053 --> 00:37:03,454
This is bad. It's an emergency!

981
00:37:03,521 --> 00:37:05,823
-I want to help him.
-Can you help him a little?

982
00:37:05,890 --> 00:37:09,327
-It's coming apart. Just use this.
-It's falling apart.

983
00:37:09,393 --> 00:37:10,928
I want to roll it for him.

984
00:37:10,995 --> 00:37:12,496
Six minutes left.

985
00:37:12,563 --> 00:37:15,333
It's hard to flatten
when you use lots of oil for the dough.

986
00:37:15,399 --> 00:37:17,401
And he has to deep-fry that too.

987
00:37:17,468 --> 00:37:21,272
-He must be reluctant to use machines.
-I guess.

988
00:37:21,339 --> 00:37:22,740
And you'll wrap the filling in it, right?

989
00:37:22,807 --> 00:37:24,742
-Yes.
-That's right.

990
00:37:24,809 --> 00:37:26,010
And deep-fry it too.

991
00:37:26,077 --> 00:37:27,612
You have to throw it in soon.

992
00:37:27,678 --> 00:37:28,980
Only five minutes left.

993
00:37:29,046 --> 00:37:30,881
Sung-ju, please check the kitchen.

994
00:37:30,948 --> 00:37:33,517
-Five minutes.
-Just deep-fry it over high heat.

995
00:37:33,584 --> 00:37:34,885
Let me go check.

996
00:37:34,952 --> 00:37:36,787
You have five minutes.

997
00:37:36,854 --> 00:37:38,422
It won't take long to deep-fry it.

998
00:37:38,489 --> 00:37:39,757
I only need three minutes.

999
00:37:39,824 --> 00:37:42,360
-You have four minutes and fifty seconds.
-Four minutes.

1000
00:37:42,426 --> 00:37:44,328
-Okay.
-Three minutes? You should start now.

1001
00:37:44,395 --> 00:37:45,329
Okay.

1002
00:37:45,396 --> 00:37:47,398
Well, everything inside is cooked.

1003
00:37:47,465 --> 00:37:50,167
-He just has to cook the wrapper.
-If it turns out well, it'll be good.

1004
00:37:50,234 --> 00:37:51,769
Can you fit everything in?

1005
00:37:51,836 --> 00:37:53,471
Yes, everything is in.

1006
00:37:53,537 --> 00:37:55,439
-Is this enough?
-Yes.

1007
00:37:55,506 --> 00:37:57,775
-It's okay. You got this.
-I got this.

1008
00:37:58,409 --> 00:37:59,577
You got this!

1009
00:37:59,644 --> 00:38:01,412
-You got this.
-Is that cheese?

1010
00:38:01,479 --> 00:38:04,048
-I think it'll work.
-Be careful.

1011
00:38:04,115 --> 00:38:05,950
-Careful.
-Okay.

1012
00:38:06,017 --> 00:38:07,852
He'll now deep-fry the dumpling.

1013
00:38:07,918 --> 00:38:09,086
It's going in.

1014
00:38:09,153 --> 00:38:11,055
-It's going in.
-The heat shouldn't be too high.

1015
00:38:11,122 --> 00:38:12,223
Don't let it pop.

1016
00:38:12,290 --> 00:38:13,824
GETTING UP

1017
00:38:13,891 --> 00:38:15,426
I don't think you should deep-fry it.

1018
00:38:15,493 --> 00:38:17,094
Wait, no!

1019
00:38:17,295 --> 00:38:18,796
EVERYONE CHIMES IN

1020
00:38:19,030 --> 00:38:20,931
SHE GETS UP TOO

1021
00:38:20,998 --> 00:38:22,099
-Stick it in oil.
-Fry it.

1022
00:38:22,166 --> 00:38:23,834
Wait, the oil isn't hot enough.

1023
00:38:23,901 --> 00:38:25,102
It's not hot enough?

1024
00:38:25,169 --> 00:38:27,004
The oil is too cold.

1025
00:38:27,071 --> 00:38:29,907
-But still, just throw it in.
-Yes, just throw it in.

1026
00:38:29,974 --> 00:38:32,343
-You have no time. Go. Use a strainer.
-Put it in first.

1027
00:38:32,610 --> 00:38:34,178
GOING IN FOR NOW

1028
00:38:34,245 --> 00:38:36,347
-Raise the heat.
-No! It's not hot enough!

1029
00:38:36,414 --> 00:38:39,250
BOTH IN DESPAIR

1030
00:38:39,317 --> 00:38:41,052
It's way too cold.

1031
00:38:41,118 --> 00:38:42,586
-He should use a pan.
-Move it into a pan!

1032
00:38:42,887 --> 00:38:44,455
CAN HE DO THIS IN 3 AND A HALF MINUTES?

1033
00:38:44,522 --> 00:38:47,024
…COMPLETELY OUT OF IT

1034
