1204
00:45:04,902 --> 00:45:06,070
It does?

1205
00:45:06,136 --> 00:45:10,140
It's just watermelon,
but it satisfies that taste.

1206
00:45:10,207 --> 00:45:12,810
It's really good.
Is it because of the cheese?

1207
00:45:12,876 --> 00:45:14,211
But there's no fish.

1208
00:45:14,278 --> 00:45:16,246
-It must be the <i>shiso.</i>
-I think so too.

1209
00:45:16,313 --> 00:45:20,484
I used lots of herbs that you had,
such as red <i>shiso</i> and mint,

1210
00:45:20,551 --> 00:45:22,586
<i>and I used them with pepper.</i>

1211
00:45:22,653 --> 00:45:25,422
So it's probably coming from
the mix of watermelon and pepper.

1212
00:45:25,489 --> 00:45:27,958
Watermelon and pepper can go together?

1213
00:45:28,025 --> 00:45:32,229
There's nothing short in this dish.

1214
00:45:32,296 --> 00:45:33,297
<i>Yaho.</i>

1215
00:45:34,198 --> 00:45:35,933
<i>-Yaho.</i>
<i>-</i>Peru, <i>yaho.</i>

1216
00:45:36,433 --> 00:45:37,868
ANOTHER BITE

1217
00:45:37,935 --> 00:45:38,936
It looks good.

1218
00:45:39,002 --> 00:45:40,003
MMM

1219
00:45:41,105 --> 00:45:42,106
IT LOOKS GOOD

1220
00:45:42,172 --> 00:45:44,141
-Is it that good?
-It's so good.

1221
00:45:44,208 --> 00:45:46,376
-So good.
-What a wonder.

1222
00:45:46,443 --> 00:45:49,313
It looks like
she might settle in South America.

1223
00:45:49,379 --> 00:45:51,715
-Let me enjoy it a little more.
-Okay.

1224
00:45:51,782 --> 00:45:55,519
-But the cruise is about to depart.
-We're about to set sail.

1225
00:45:55,586 --> 00:45:56,553
I knew it.

1226
00:45:56,620 --> 00:45:59,423
-It's off to a great start.
-I see.

1227
00:45:59,490 --> 00:46:01,492
Next up on your tour is Europe.

1228
00:46:01,558 --> 00:46:02,693
The cruise is departing.

1229
00:46:02,760 --> 00:46:05,429
He turned the zucchini into steak.

1230
00:46:05,496 --> 00:46:07,865
-Whoa.
-That's a zucchini steak.

1231
00:46:07,931 --> 00:46:09,133
What's at the bottom?

1232
00:46:09,199 --> 00:46:13,537
It's something I made
with tomatoes and other vegetables.

1233
00:46:13,604 --> 00:46:15,806
How did he do this in that short of time?

1234
00:46:16,974 --> 00:46:19,977
WILL SHE ALSO ENJOY THE EUROPEAN FLAVOR?

1235
00:46:20,410 --> 00:46:21,979
AN AUTOMATIC SMILE

1236
00:46:22,045 --> 00:46:23,680
Is the zucchini okay?

1237
00:46:24,381 --> 00:46:25,415
TOTALLY OKAY♥

1238
00:46:25,783 --> 00:46:28,786
OKAY, EUROPE♥

1239
00:46:28,852 --> 00:46:31,722
It's not overly done.

1240
00:46:31,789 --> 00:46:34,892
The zucchini itself is already delicious,

1241
00:46:34,958 --> 00:46:37,394
but he added Romanesco broccoli here

1242
00:46:37,461 --> 00:46:39,096
and gave it a crunchy texture,

1243
00:46:39,163 --> 00:46:40,998
finishing off with the tender cheese.

1244
00:46:41,732 --> 00:46:46,036
On top of that, the tomato sauce
makes up for the missing flavors,

1245
00:46:46,103 --> 00:46:47,237
so it's very delicious.

1246
00:46:47,304 --> 00:46:48,939
Lastly, Korea.

1247
00:46:49,006 --> 00:46:50,974
-Supersweet corn <i>beomuri.</i>
<i>-Beomuri.</i>

1248
00:46:51,041 --> 00:46:52,176
Ta-da.

1249
00:46:52,843 --> 00:46:54,011
How do I eat this?

1250
00:46:54,077 --> 00:46:55,679
I'll have to head there once again.

1251
00:46:55,746 --> 00:46:57,247
-Again?
-Again?

1252
00:46:57,314 --> 00:46:59,416
There's a way to eat it.

1253
00:46:59,483 --> 00:47:02,953
I prepared this, hoping you'd
feel the touch of a grandmother

1254
00:47:03,020 --> 00:47:05,189
as you recover from your trip
to South America and Europe.

1255
00:47:05,255 --> 00:47:06,190
Scrape it like this.

1256
00:47:06,256 --> 00:47:09,259
-You're supposed to scrape it.
-Like that?

1257
00:47:09,326 --> 00:47:11,728
It's called <i>beomuri</i>
because you mix it as you eat it.

1258
00:47:11,795 --> 00:47:13,730
It's often made with mugwort.

1259
00:47:13,797 --> 00:47:15,465
-Yes, mugwort <i>beomuri.</i>
-Right.

1260
00:47:15,532 --> 00:47:17,968
Scrape it, put it down,

1261
00:47:18,635 --> 00:47:20,804
and sprinkle a bit of honey on it.

1262
00:47:20,871 --> 00:47:22,873
-It looks good.
-Supersweet corn <i>beomuri.</i>

1263
00:47:22,940 --> 00:47:24,474
-That looks good.
-Corn <i>beomuri.</i>

1264
00:47:24,541 --> 00:47:26,376
Try the <i>beomuri</i> alone first.

1265
00:47:26,443 --> 00:47:28,846
-And then with honey.
-I can smell it.

1266
00:47:28,912 --> 00:47:31,949
It really feels like
I returned home after a trip,

1267
00:47:32,015 --> 00:47:34,084
and I'm at my grandmother's house.

1268
00:47:34,151 --> 00:47:36,153
She's at her grandmother's place.

1269
00:47:36,220 --> 00:47:40,123
He powdered the rice himself
and made the <i>beomuri.</i>

1270
00:47:41,024 --> 00:47:42,826
CHOKING UP

1271
00:47:42,893 --> 00:47:44,061
Are you crying?

1272
00:47:44,127 --> 00:47:45,863
She met her grandma after a while.

1273
00:47:47,231 --> 00:47:49,132
SAVORING WITHOUT A WORD

1274
00:47:49,199 --> 00:47:50,734
Grandma.

1275
00:47:50,801 --> 00:47:53,670
CHOKING UP TOO

1276
00:47:53,737 --> 00:47:55,405
It's so good.

1277
00:47:56,773 --> 00:47:58,775
I also love <i>tteok,</i>

1278
00:47:58,842 --> 00:48:01,612
but I can't eat it often because of carbs.

1279
00:48:01,678 --> 00:48:04,248
But I'd eat this
when I return from a trip.

1280
00:48:05,849 --> 00:48:07,951
Try it with some honey.

1281
00:48:08,018 --> 00:48:09,586
Is that just regular honey?

1282
00:48:09,653 --> 00:48:11,355
She had truffle honey,

1283
00:48:11,421 --> 00:48:13,790
and truffle goes very well with corn.

1284
00:48:15,058 --> 00:48:16,360
He used all the right things.

1285
00:48:16,426 --> 00:48:18,095
My gosh.

1286
00:48:18,161 --> 00:48:20,163
Truffle honey and corn.

1287
00:48:20,230 --> 00:48:21,298
Look at this, Sooyoung.

1288
00:48:22,466 --> 00:48:24,568
-I want to try it so badly.
-Let me try it first.

1289
00:48:24,935 --> 00:48:26,503
OH, YOU'RE SUCH A TEASE

1290
00:48:26,970 --> 00:48:28,772
HOW WELL DO THEY GO TOGETHER?

1291
00:48:28,839 --> 00:48:30,574
It'll be exhilarating in your mouth.

1292
00:48:30,641 --> 00:48:34,077
-It must be sweet.
-Is it different with honey?

1293
00:48:34,144 --> 00:48:35,779
The truffle smell hits me hard.

1294
00:48:37,047 --> 00:48:39,449
It's very sweet and very delicious.

1295
00:48:39,516 --> 00:48:43,053
Like dipping <i>garae-tteok,</i> bar rice cake,
in grain syrup.

1296
00:48:43,120 --> 00:48:44,655
Even I can smell the truffle.

1297
00:48:44,721 --> 00:48:45,923
-Right?
-Yes.

1298
00:48:45,989 --> 00:48:47,224
SHOWING OFF

1299
00:48:48,225 --> 00:48:49,259
She's showing it off so much.

1300
00:48:50,928 --> 00:48:53,330
Gosh, it looks so good.

1301
00:48:53,397 --> 00:48:54,932
It really is.

1302
00:48:54,998 --> 00:48:56,233
Korea, <i>yaho?</i>

1303
00:48:56,300 --> 00:48:59,970
-<i>Yaho!</i>
-It's a <i>yaho</i> for Korea too.

1304
00:49:00,037 --> 00:49:01,538
Next, Brazil.

1305
00:49:01,605 --> 00:49:02,739
Let's visit Brazil now.

1306
00:49:04,007 --> 00:49:06,143
PASTEL FILLED
WITH HANGING TENDER AND VEGETABLES

1307
00:49:06,543 --> 00:49:09,212
SERVED WITH SMOKY SUPERSWEET CORN

1308
00:49:09,846 --> 00:49:12,916
AND SALSA TO ENHANCE THE PASTEL'S FLAVOR

1309
00:49:13,617 --> 00:49:17,120
BRAZILIAN COCKTAIL, CAIPIRINHA

1310
00:49:17,688 --> 00:49:21,325
DIRECTOR YOO YONG-WOOK'S BRAZIL, YAREU

1311
00:49:21,959 --> 00:49:23,327
I'm feeling anxious.

1312
00:49:23,393 --> 00:49:25,062
-Why?
-Why?

1313
00:49:25,128 --> 00:49:26,229
This is just my taste.

1314
00:49:27,264 --> 00:49:28,999
-Because it's your taste.
-<i>Yareu.</i>

1315
00:49:29,066 --> 00:49:32,869
Everyone, get ready to party
until 3:00 a.m. tonight.

1316
00:49:33,170 --> 00:49:34,571
OH, WE'D "LOVE" THAT

1317
00:49:34,638 --> 00:49:36,940
She's getting started now.

1318
00:49:37,808 --> 00:49:38,976
I have work early tomorrow.

1319
00:49:39,042 --> 00:49:40,143
You still can't leave.

1320
00:49:40,344 --> 00:49:41,411
I ACTUALLY WOULD LOVE TO

1321
00:49:41,478 --> 00:49:43,480
-What's the first dish?
-In that tiny dish,

1322
00:49:43,547 --> 00:49:45,916
I prepared salsa, made with tomatoes,

1323
00:49:45,983 --> 00:49:48,318
<i>cucumbers, red onion, and salsa verde.</i>

1324
00:49:48,385 --> 00:49:50,520
Try the <i>pastel</i> with the sauce

1325
00:49:50,587 --> 00:49:53,156
and pick up the corn
to enjoy it like a rib.

1326
00:49:53,223 --> 00:49:55,959
-Okay.
-And have the Caipirinha as you like.

1327
00:49:56,026 --> 00:49:58,095
-Should I mix the sauce?
-Yes, please.

1328
00:49:58,161 --> 00:50:00,263
That sauce was really good.

1329
00:50:00,330 --> 00:50:01,598
Mix it all the way through.

1330
00:50:01,665 --> 00:50:03,066
-What's it like?
-It looks good.

1331
00:50:03,133 --> 00:50:03,967
It was good.

1332
00:50:04,034 --> 00:50:06,403
I really love tacos.

1333
00:50:06,470 --> 00:50:08,205
So it feels like that sauce.

1334
00:50:08,271 --> 00:50:09,873
That actually looks so good.

1335
00:50:09,940 --> 00:50:11,742
-I'll try it with lots of sauce.
-Okay.

1336
00:50:11,808 --> 00:50:14,611
-Have it with lots of sauce.
-Lots of sauce.

1337
00:50:14,678 --> 00:50:16,980
-It looks so good.
-My gosh.

1338
00:50:17,047 --> 00:50:19,149
-Hey, salsa.
-Brazilian flavor.

1339
00:50:19,616 --> 00:50:21,651
A BIG BITE

1340
00:50:24,821 --> 00:50:26,556
SMILING

1341
00:50:26,623 --> 00:50:28,058
Is it good? Do you like it?

1342
00:50:28,892 --> 00:50:30,827
Director Yong-wook, what is this?
It's so good.

1343
00:50:32,295 --> 00:50:34,731
-He was being a crybaby.
-What a crybaby.

1344
00:50:34,798 --> 00:50:37,667
-It's really good.
-It's really good!

1345
00:50:37,734 --> 00:50:38,935
My gosh.

1346
00:50:39,002 --> 00:50:40,570
<i>-Yareu!</i>
<i>-Yareu!</i>

1347
00:50:40,637 --> 00:50:42,506
<i>-Yareu!</i>
-Come on.

1348
00:50:42,572 --> 00:50:45,275
<i>-Yareu!</i>
<i>-Yareu!</i>

1349
00:50:45,342 --> 00:50:46,543
It's the death of <i>yareu</i> as a meme.

1350
00:50:46,777 --> 00:50:48,712
FAREWELL, YAREU…

1351
00:50:48,779 --> 00:50:50,847
Does the sauce play a big part?

1352
00:50:50,914 --> 00:50:53,483
Tacos can be bland without salsa, right?

1353
00:50:53,550 --> 00:50:55,652
-Yes.
-This sauce is so good.

1354
00:50:55,719 --> 00:50:56,620
-So you need it.
-Yes.

1355
00:50:56,686 --> 00:51:00,057
Also, he seemed unsure
when he deep-fried this,

1356
00:51:00,123 --> 00:51:03,427
-but it has that crunch to it.
-It does? I'm relieved.

1357
00:51:03,493 --> 00:51:07,497
At times, when the skin is too thick,
it feels too much when I eat it.

1358
00:51:07,564 --> 00:51:10,067
But it breaks apart with a crunch,

1359
00:51:10,133 --> 00:51:13,136
mixing nicely with the filling.

1360
00:51:13,403 --> 00:51:14,671
PERFECT FLAVOR AND TEXTURE

1361
00:51:14,738 --> 00:51:15,939
It's so good.

1362
00:51:16,139 --> 00:51:17,808
HAPPY

1363
00:51:17,874 --> 00:51:20,410
The cob is still attached,
so don't eat that.

1364
00:51:20,477 --> 00:51:21,645
That must be fantastic.

1365
00:51:22,712 --> 00:51:24,281
Hmm?

1366
00:51:24,347 --> 00:51:25,949
How did you grill this?

1367
00:51:26,016 --> 00:51:29,186
I didn't want to ruin
the corn's natural sweetness,

1368
00:51:29,252 --> 00:51:30,253
<i>so I mixed it with olive oil,</i>

1369
00:51:30,320 --> 00:51:33,156
<i>meat seasoning, hot sauce,</i>
<i>and red pepper powder.</i>

1370
00:51:33,223 --> 00:51:35,492
-Hot sauce.
-Hot sauce.

1371
00:51:35,559 --> 00:51:37,561
It's really good.

1372
00:51:37,627 --> 00:51:39,696
When I go surfing in Bali,

1373
00:51:39,763 --> 00:51:44,167
<i>there's a snack you can buy,</i>
<i>corn on the cob with seasoning.</i>

1374
00:51:44,234 --> 00:51:46,336
It reminds me of that. It's so good.

1375
00:51:46,403 --> 00:51:47,904
It looks good.

1376
00:51:47,971 --> 00:51:50,740
I'm so excited for this cocktail.

1377
00:51:50,807 --> 00:51:53,243
Yes, that's something she would love.

1378
00:51:53,310 --> 00:51:54,978
It's like the water in her fridge.

1379
00:51:55,045 --> 00:51:57,814
-He made it with what's in the water.
-You're right.

1380
00:51:57,881 --> 00:51:59,182
What's it called again?

1381
00:51:59,249 --> 00:52:00,083
-Caipirinha.
-Caipirinha.

1382
00:52:00,150 --> 00:52:01,184
Caipirinha.

1383
00:52:01,251 --> 00:52:02,552
-Caipirinha.
-It actually means

1384
00:52:02,619 --> 00:52:04,454
-"…little country girl."
-It does?

1385
00:52:04,521 --> 00:52:06,790
WILL SHE LOVE THE FLAVOR TOO?

1386
00:52:07,791 --> 00:52:09,426
Caipirinha.

1387
00:52:10,127 --> 00:52:11,394
NODDING QUIETLY?

1388
00:52:11,461 --> 00:52:14,064
-She may not like it.
-It's a Brazilian cocktail, after all.

1389
00:52:14,131 --> 00:52:16,399
DOES SHE NOT LIKE IT?

1390
00:52:16,466 --> 00:52:17,767
It's really weird.

1391
00:52:17,834 --> 00:52:18,935
It doesn't taste like liquor.

1392
00:52:20,137 --> 00:52:21,705
It feels like he added

1393
00:52:21,771 --> 00:52:23,640
lots of lime into the water that I make.

1394
00:52:25,075 --> 00:52:26,209
I can't taste the alcohol.

1395
00:52:26,276 --> 00:52:28,645
That's why it's dangerous.

1396
00:52:28,712 --> 00:52:30,347
The first time
my Brazilian friend made that for me,

1397
00:52:30,413 --> 00:52:32,549
I kept drinking it
because I couldn't taste the alcohol,

1398
00:52:32,616 --> 00:52:34,718
and I was out after just one hour.

1399
00:52:34,784 --> 00:52:35,785
It's like that?

1400
00:52:35,852 --> 00:52:38,588
It sounds fascinating,
and now I can't wait to get tipsy.

1401
00:52:38,655 --> 00:52:40,390
-You can't wait?
-Right.

1402
00:52:40,457 --> 00:52:41,925
He said it can make you drunk.

1403
00:52:41,992 --> 00:52:43,460
No one is going home.

1404
00:52:43,994 --> 00:52:45,629
EMERGENCY FOR HIM

1405
00:52:45,929 --> 00:52:48,198
LET'S HURRY BEFORE SHE GETS DRUNK…

1406
00:52:48,265 --> 00:52:51,001
-But it was really good.
-I see.

1407
00:52:51,067 --> 00:52:53,270
It seems like she wants to be a bit tipsy.

1408
00:52:53,336 --> 00:52:55,672
-No, I was joking.
-All right.

1409
00:52:55,739 --> 00:52:56,973
I can't drink much.

1410
00:52:57,307 --> 00:52:59,476
ABOUT THIS MUCH?

1411
00:52:59,543 --> 00:53:02,045
Now, she'll decide right away.

1412
00:53:02,112 --> 00:53:04,281
-Please decide.
-What do I do?

1413
00:53:04,347 --> 00:53:06,416
<i>Press one to choose Jong-won</i>

1414
00:53:06,483 --> 00:53:08,285
and two to choose Yong-wook.

1415
00:53:08,351 --> 00:53:11,288
Please make your decision
and press the button.

1416
00:53:12,255 --> 00:53:13,390
PRESS

1417
00:53:14,658 --> 00:53:16,993
-I pressed one.
-She pressed one.

1418
00:53:17,060 --> 00:53:19,930
Where will the cruise ship dock?

1419
00:53:19,996 --> 00:53:22,432
Let's find out the result.

1420
00:53:23,900 --> 00:53:26,069
This round was titled, "Okay, Land."

1421
00:53:26,136 --> 00:53:28,838
Will it be Yoo Yong-wook's second win?

1422
00:53:28,905 --> 00:53:31,775
Or will it be Jong-won,
the current first place?

1423
00:53:31,841 --> 00:53:34,778
Who did Jung-hwa choose?

1424
00:53:34,844 --> 00:53:36,913
Who did she choose?

1425
00:53:36,980 --> 00:53:38,582
-All right.
-Who got the okay?

1426
00:53:38,648 --> 00:53:40,283
Will Yong-wook catch the big fish?

1427
00:53:40,350 --> 00:53:43,053
YOO YONG-WOOK WINS

1428
00:53:43,253 --> 00:53:45,222
DIRECTOR YOO YONG-WOOK'S
BRAZIL, YAREU WINS

1429
00:53:45,288 --> 00:53:47,991
-Are you serious?
-Thank you.

1430
00:53:48,058 --> 00:53:49,359
I can't believe this!

1431
00:53:49,426 --> 00:53:51,728
THIS IS HUGE

1432
00:53:51,928 --> 00:53:53,630
EMERGENCY

1433
00:53:53,697 --> 00:53:55,732
He takes Jong-won down.

1434
00:53:55,799 --> 00:53:57,934
-He defeated him.
-You won against first place.

1435
00:53:58,001 --> 00:54:00,804
I didn't want to leave this place.

1436
00:54:02,706 --> 00:54:04,441
She got off and loved it.

1437
00:54:04,507 --> 00:54:07,277
Chef Jong-won's dishes

1438
00:54:07,344 --> 00:54:11,381
<i>were flawlessly beautiful and delicious.</i>

1439
00:54:11,448 --> 00:54:14,417
But I had to travel to many places,

1440
00:54:14,484 --> 00:54:16,720
so I decided to anchor here

1441
00:54:16,786 --> 00:54:19,456
-and drink this until 3:00 a.m.
-It was the drink.

1442
00:54:19,522 --> 00:54:22,425
-The Caipirinha played a big part.
-Until 3:00 a.m.

1443
00:54:22,492 --> 00:54:25,795
I got to do this,
thanks to many people's help.

1444
00:54:25,862 --> 00:54:30,066
I didn't think I'd win,
so I didn't think of what to say.

1445
00:54:30,133 --> 00:54:31,935
He looks happier now than at <i>CCW2.</i>

1446
00:54:33,570 --> 00:54:35,472
Thank you for enjoying my dish.

1447
00:54:35,538 --> 00:54:38,808
CONGRATULATIONS

1448
00:54:38,875 --> 00:54:40,410
It's his second win.

1449
00:54:40,477 --> 00:54:43,980
He fought twice and won twice,
a win rate of 100%.

1450
00:54:44,180 --> 00:54:46,016
JONG-WON'S THE BEST
YONG-WOOK'S THE BEST

1451
00:54:46,082 --> 00:54:49,052
-Congratulations!
-Congratulations!

1452
00:54:49,119 --> 00:54:50,887
Thank you.

1453
00:54:51,087 --> 00:54:52,422
CONGRATS

1454
00:54:53,056 --> 00:54:54,891
Let's take ten.

1455
00:54:54,958 --> 00:54:55,959
AFTER THE FIRST ROUND

1456
00:54:56,026 --> 00:54:58,295
-Let me try that.
-That was fun.

1457
00:54:58,361 --> 00:55:00,163
It was really good.

1458
00:55:00,497 --> 00:55:02,599
GOING AFTER THE SUPERSWEET CORN BEOMURI

1459
00:55:03,733 --> 00:55:04,968
-<i>Beomuri'</i>s good.
-Whoa.

1460
00:55:05,302 --> 00:55:06,336
IT'S SO GOOD

1461
00:55:06,403 --> 00:55:07,704
This is insane.

1462
00:55:08,571 --> 00:55:09,973
DIPPING IN THE HONEY

1463
00:55:10,206 --> 00:55:11,341
CHUCKLING

1464
00:55:11,408 --> 00:55:13,510
-Do you have water for <i>ramyeon?</i>
-Everything's good.

1465
00:55:13,576 --> 00:55:14,978
ALL SAYING WHATEVER THEY WANT TO

1466
00:55:15,245 --> 00:55:18,348
SOOYOUNG AND THE TUBBY TWINS
ENJOYED THE REST

1467
00:55:18,415 --> 00:55:22,419
<i>Let's play the second cook-off</i>
<i>with Jung-hwa's ingredients.</i>

1468
00:55:22,485 --> 00:55:24,921
Today, they are legitimate partners,

1469
00:55:24,988 --> 00:55:27,057
Chef Sam Kim and Chef Jung Ho-young.

1470
00:55:27,123 --> 00:55:29,859
The orthodox and the unorthodox
are now on the same boat,

1471
00:55:29,926 --> 00:55:33,530
Webtoon Artist Kim Poong
and Chef Lee Moon-jung.

1472
00:55:33,596 --> 00:55:37,434
Poong has played
a team round with Hyun-suk…

1473
00:55:37,500 --> 00:55:39,669
-Yes.
-…but it's your first with Moon-jung.

1474
00:55:39,736 --> 00:55:41,471
-Yes.
-Do you feel more at ease now?

1475
00:55:41,538 --> 00:55:43,173
Chef Hyun-suk

1476
00:55:43,239 --> 00:55:44,908
also isn't half bad, right?

1477
00:55:44,974 --> 00:55:47,477
THE CHEF OF 30 YEARS ISN'T HALF BAD

1478
00:55:47,544 --> 00:55:49,212
-He has a few tricks.
-Yes.

1479
00:55:49,279 --> 00:55:50,613
"A few tricks"?

1480
00:55:50,680 --> 00:55:52,582
Thank you, Chef Poong.

1481
00:55:52,649 --> 00:55:55,952
-I know that he tries hard.
-So jealous. I want his approval too.

1482
00:55:56,019 --> 00:55:59,689
Actually, Chef Moon-jung

1483
00:55:59,756 --> 00:56:01,658
is a certified master chef.

1484
00:56:01,725 --> 00:56:02,926
-A master chef.
-Yes, she is.

1485
00:56:02,992 --> 00:56:04,661
A certified master chef means

1486
00:56:04,728 --> 00:56:07,664
-that she's pretty much a technician.
-Yes.

1487
00:56:07,731 --> 00:56:10,133
I've never felt
more at ease to play as a team.

1488
00:56:10,200 --> 00:56:13,269
We were saying Poong
and Moon-jung make a good team,

1489
00:56:13,336 --> 00:56:16,106
but the same goes
for Sam and Ho-young as well.

1490
00:56:16,172 --> 00:56:17,907
They're guaranteed to have good teamwork.

1491
00:56:17,974 --> 00:56:21,711
This is our second time
actually cooking as a team,

1492
00:56:21,778 --> 00:56:24,414
but we were pretty much always a team.

1493
00:56:24,481 --> 00:56:25,615
Yes, I've seen you two work together.

1494
00:56:25,682 --> 00:56:27,117
Can you melt this?

1495
00:56:27,384 --> 00:56:29,619
ALWAYS HELPING WITHOUT COMPLAINING,

1496
00:56:29,686 --> 00:56:32,322
SAM KIM, THE KITCHEN TOOLMAN

1497
00:56:32,889 --> 00:56:34,391
So I feel confident today.

1498
00:56:34,457 --> 00:56:35,992
I knew it.

1499
00:56:36,826 --> 00:56:39,863
TEAM HO-YOUNG & SAM:
LOW-SUGAR BREAD, SLICED CHEESE, BRIE

1500
00:56:39,929 --> 00:56:42,999
SQUID, SHRIMP, SCALLOP, CHUCK FLAP TAIL,
ANCHOVY AEKJEOT, HOT SAUCE, AND MORE

1501
00:56:43,066 --> 00:56:44,801
What are they making?

1502
00:56:44,868 --> 00:56:48,805
We'll make something so delicious
that you wouldn't even notice

1503
00:56:48,872 --> 00:56:51,307
-that it's low-carb, low-sugar.
-I thought, "Why the bowls?"

1504
00:56:51,374 --> 00:56:52,575
For the first dish,

1505
00:56:52,642 --> 00:56:55,478
you said you couldn't eat noodles often
even though you liked them,

1506
00:56:55,545 --> 00:56:57,213
and you had thin glass noodles.

1507
00:56:57,280 --> 00:56:59,282
So we'll make
a spicy, sour, and sweet dish

1508
00:56:59,349 --> 00:57:01,885
just like <i>yum woon sen</i> in Thai cuisine.

1509
00:57:01,951 --> 00:57:04,854
-That sounds good.
-So we'll give it that strong flavor.

1510
00:57:04,921 --> 00:57:06,623
Sam will take the next dish.

1511
00:57:06,689 --> 00:57:08,491
It's called Philly cheese steak.

1512
00:57:08,558 --> 00:57:10,427
-Philly cheese steak.
-It's a sandwich

1513
00:57:10,493 --> 00:57:13,530
with meat and cheese inside.

1514
00:57:13,596 --> 00:57:16,199
It's considered pretty heavy,
but I'll serve it with <i>râpées</i>

1515
00:57:16,266 --> 00:57:18,868
made with zucchinis and carrots.

1516
00:57:18,935 --> 00:57:21,838
So that'll add
some healthiness to the dish.

1517
00:57:21,905 --> 00:57:24,641
Since you can't usually eat fast food,

1518
00:57:24,707 --> 00:57:28,077
we'll make something you can eat
without worrying too much.

1519
00:57:28,144 --> 00:57:31,047
-Who's going first? Ho-young?
-I'll go first.

1520
00:57:31,114 --> 00:57:32,816
-And then Sam.
-Then Sam.

1521
00:57:32,882 --> 00:57:36,820
My goal is to tag him in
at around six minutes.

1522
00:57:36,886 --> 00:57:39,622
He has a big role to play,

1523
00:57:39,689 --> 00:57:42,392
so if we lose, it's his fault.

1524
00:57:42,459 --> 00:57:44,327
"But if it goes well, kudos to me."

1525
00:57:45,161 --> 00:57:47,697
TEAM POONG & MOON-JUNG:
DOENJANG, SOYMILK NOODLES, RED ONION

1526
00:57:47,764 --> 00:57:50,333
BUTTON MUSHROOM, CUCUMBER,
CARROT, CABBAGE, TOMATO, AND MORE

1527
00:57:50,400 --> 00:57:54,103
Poong and Moon-jung, you're up too.
What are you making?

1528
00:57:54,170 --> 00:57:58,775
-The name of our dish is…
-There it is.

1529
00:57:58,842 --> 00:58:00,276
-They'll do something.
-They'll do it.

1530
00:58:00,844 --> 00:58:02,879
-Chinese Poison.
-Poison.

1531
00:58:02,946 --> 00:58:04,481
Chinese Poison.

1532
00:58:04,547 --> 00:58:05,982
So what are you making?

1533
00:58:06,049 --> 00:58:09,719
We caught on to something
you mentioned earlier.

1534
00:58:09,786 --> 00:58:12,689
<i>"Jjajangmyeon."</i>
You said you were craving it.

1535
00:58:12,755 --> 00:58:13,623
<i>Jjajangmyeon?</i>

1536
00:58:13,690 --> 00:58:18,194
We'll make low-carb,
low-sugar <i>jjajangmyeon</i> for you.

1537
00:58:18,261 --> 00:58:19,529
Can you do that?

1538
00:58:19,596 --> 00:58:21,064
Isn't it high-carb, high-sugar?

1539
00:58:21,130 --> 00:58:22,999
To suit the theme of low-carb, low-sugar,

1540
00:58:23,066 --> 00:58:26,302
we'll use your mother's homemade <i>doenjang</i>.

1541
00:58:26,369 --> 00:58:28,938
JJAJANGMYEON WITH DOENJANG?

1542
00:58:29,005 --> 00:58:31,174
-<i>Doenjang jjajangmyeon</i> is great.
-It is.

1543
00:58:31,241 --> 00:58:34,344
We'll make the sauce
with homemade <i>doenjang.</i>

1544
00:58:34,410 --> 00:58:35,845
-That's such a wonder.
-And one more.

1545
00:58:35,912 --> 00:58:37,213
-Menbosha.
-Menbosha?

1546
00:58:37,280 --> 00:58:39,015
-Menbosha?
-My gosh.

1547
00:58:39,082 --> 00:58:40,884
-She's in trouble.
-I'll make menbosha

1548
00:58:40,950 --> 00:58:44,554
with the sourdough bread you had.

1549
00:58:44,621 --> 00:58:48,324
I'll slice that thinly
and make it low-carb.

1550
00:58:48,391 --> 00:58:50,260
So who's up first?

1551
00:58:50,326 --> 00:58:51,995
-I'm up first.
-I knew it.

1552
00:58:52,061 --> 00:58:53,196
She's quick.

1553
00:58:53,263 --> 00:58:54,864
So Poong will finish?

1554
00:58:54,931 --> 00:58:56,299
-Yes.
-By any chance,

1555
00:58:56,366 --> 00:58:58,701
will Moon-jung use 14 minutes
and you, one minute?

1556
00:58:58,768 --> 00:59:00,270
What are you talking about?

1557
00:59:00,336 --> 00:59:01,471
It's 14 minutes and 50 seconds for her.

1558
00:59:01,771 --> 00:59:04,140
SEEMS LIKE HE REALLY WANTS TO DO THAT

1559
00:59:04,207 --> 00:59:05,909
Serve and ring the bell, that's his job.

1560
00:59:05,975 --> 00:59:07,877
All right, now.

1561
00:59:07,944 --> 00:59:11,047
Let us begin the team round.

1562
00:59:11,114 --> 00:59:14,851
Witch With a Wok
meets Cook of the Darkness.

1563
00:59:14,918 --> 00:59:19,055
The crazy Chinese siblings,
Kim Poong and Lee Moon-jung.

1564
00:59:19,122 --> 00:59:21,591
A chef and a kitchen toolman.

1565
00:59:21,658 --> 00:59:25,261
-"A kitchen toolman"?
-"A kitchen toolman"?

1566
00:59:25,328 --> 00:59:27,196
The star-crazed brothers,

1567
00:59:27,263 --> 00:59:30,300
Ho-young and Sam are in this round.

1568
00:59:30,366 --> 00:59:32,669
It's our first two-on-two tag team round.

1569
00:59:32,735 --> 00:59:34,237
Who'll rise as the winners?

1570
00:59:34,304 --> 00:59:37,373
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1571
00:59:37,440 --> 00:59:38,341
Let's do this!

1572
00:59:38,875 --> 00:59:40,043
HO-YOUNG STARTS!

1573
00:59:40,410 --> 00:59:41,744
MOON-JUNG STARTS!

1574
00:59:41,811 --> 00:59:43,580
POONG'S OUT, SAM'S OUT

1575
00:59:44,280 --> 00:59:46,282
Sam and Poong, sit back down.

1576
00:59:46,349 --> 00:59:47,684
She sharpens the knife quickly too.

1577
00:59:47,750 --> 00:59:49,752
Ho-young versus Moon-jung.

1578
00:59:49,819 --> 00:59:51,254
Okay.

1579
00:59:51,321 --> 00:59:53,456
That's good. Start right away.

1580
00:59:53,523 --> 00:59:55,959
-Is that the <i>jjajang </i>sauce?
-She's using <i>doenjang.</i>

1581
00:59:56,025 --> 00:59:57,827
It's her mother's homemade <i>doenjang.</i>

1582
00:59:57,894 --> 01:00:00,330
-Yes.
-She's frying homemade <i>doenjang.</i>

1583
01:00:00,396 --> 01:00:02,131
You can make <i>jjajang </i>sauce with <i>doenjang.</i>

1584
01:00:02,198 --> 01:00:03,333
It's really dark.

1585
01:00:03,399 --> 01:00:04,467
She's frying it like <i>chunjang.</i>

1586
01:00:04,534 --> 01:00:07,136
-It's Gangwon-do style.
-It's so dark.

1587
01:00:07,203 --> 01:00:08,938
-And it has a lot of umami.
-Yes.

1588
01:00:09,005 --> 01:00:11,541
-It's been fermented for a while.
-Yes.

1589
01:00:11,608 --> 01:00:14,544
-She's frying <i>doenjang </i>like it's <i>chunjang.</i>
-The same way.

1590
01:00:14,611 --> 01:00:15,612
That's cool.

1591
01:00:15,678 --> 01:00:18,214
-It's hard to imagine.
-Will it turn out like <i>jjajang </i>sauce?

1592
01:00:18,281 --> 01:00:20,116
Korean-style <i>jjajang </i>sauce
is made with Korean <i>chunjang.</i>

1593
01:00:20,183 --> 01:00:23,586
But the original Chinese <i>jjajang</i> sauce
is similar to <i>doenjang,</i> like that one.

1594
01:00:23,653 --> 01:00:25,154
-I see.
-Is that the case?

1595
01:00:25,221 --> 01:00:27,657
That's such a professional move
that Poong could never display.

1596
01:00:27,724 --> 01:00:29,158
That's why she's a master chef.

1597
01:00:29,225 --> 01:00:30,426
Wow.

1598
01:00:30,693 --> 01:00:32,195
HO-YOUNG FIGHTS BACK

1599
01:00:32,261 --> 01:00:33,529
-Cheese.
-For the Philly cheese steak?

1600
01:00:33,596 --> 01:00:35,231
-The Philly cheese steak.
-Cheese steak.

1601
01:00:35,298 --> 01:00:37,233
They'll use carrots too.

1602
01:00:37,300 --> 01:00:39,268
-Jung-hwa loves all those.
-I do.

1603
01:00:39,335 --> 01:00:41,571
-Carrots, what she eats every day.
-Yes.

1604
01:00:42,805 --> 01:00:44,607
STARTING ON THE YUM WOON SEN

1605
01:00:44,907 --> 01:00:46,876
SQUID

1606
01:00:47,510 --> 01:00:49,646
PERFECTLY CUT SQUID!

1607
01:00:50,580 --> 01:00:51,547
RAW SHRIMP MEAT

1608
01:00:51,614 --> 01:00:54,283
-Ho-young is…
-He just scored the squid.

1609
01:00:54,350 --> 01:00:55,985
He's using supersweet corn.

1610
01:00:57,220 --> 01:00:59,822
2. BLANCH SQUID, SHRIMP, AND CORN

1611
01:01:00,089 --> 01:01:01,691
MOON-JUNG STARTS ON THE MENBOSHA

1612
01:01:01,758 --> 01:01:03,459
Shrimp.

1613
01:01:03,526 --> 01:01:05,061
The menbosha.

1614
01:01:05,128 --> 01:01:08,064
Starch, salt, ground pepper.

1615
01:01:08,131 --> 01:01:10,333
All right. Okay.

1616
01:01:10,400 --> 01:01:12,035
Egg whites. What about those?

1617
01:01:12,869 --> 01:01:13,703
EGG WHITES

1618
01:01:14,170 --> 01:01:16,806
Is it okay for him to coach his partner?

1619
01:01:16,873 --> 01:01:18,708
Who cares? I'm just talking.

1620
01:01:18,775 --> 01:01:21,377
-It's okay.
-You can talk.

1621
01:01:21,444 --> 01:01:23,513
-You have to stir this.
-You're a team.

1622
01:01:23,579 --> 01:01:25,748
-You just can't help in person.
-Grind it hard.

1623
01:01:25,815 --> 01:01:27,850
MENBOSHA FILLING IS DONE!

1624
01:01:27,917 --> 01:01:29,686
He's peeling the zucchini.

1625
01:01:31,254 --> 01:01:33,723
-Rotating and peeling.
-Peeling something like that

1626
01:01:33,790 --> 01:01:36,059
is something Western chefs
have nothing on Japanese chefs.

1627
01:01:36,125 --> 01:01:38,294
Yes, Japanese chefs are so good at that.

1628
01:01:38,361 --> 01:01:40,563
-I see.
-That's really tough to do.

1629
01:01:40,630 --> 01:01:41,631
-At once.
-It's not easy.

1630
01:01:41,698 --> 01:01:42,699
And it's thin too.

1631
01:01:42,765 --> 01:01:45,401
He's good at peeling stuff.

1632
01:01:45,468 --> 01:01:48,471
You'll find that Ho-young
is always trying to peel me apart.

1633
01:01:48,538 --> 01:01:49,739
OH…

1634
01:01:50,673 --> 01:01:51,674
Sorry.

1635
01:01:51,741 --> 01:01:54,410
You laughed at everything Jong-won said.
Why the discrimination?

1636
01:01:54,477 --> 01:01:56,412
YOU'RE SO FUNNY, HYUN-SUK

1637
01:01:56,479 --> 01:01:57,613
Are you using the peel?

1638
01:01:57,680 --> 01:02:00,416
Yes, the peel has
a nice, sweet flavor to it,

1639
01:02:00,483 --> 01:02:02,218
so I'm using just this part.

1640
01:02:02,285 --> 01:02:03,820
You're fast. Excellent.

1641
01:02:04,187 --> 01:02:06,289
MOON-JUNG OVERPOWERS HIM WITH HER SPEED

1642
01:02:06,355 --> 01:02:08,057
Nice speed!

1643
01:02:08,558 --> 01:02:10,693
RED ONION

1644
01:02:11,360 --> 01:02:13,329
THE CLASS OF THE MASTER CHEF!

1645
01:02:13,396 --> 01:02:14,797
She's so cool.

1646
01:02:15,031 --> 01:02:16,265
DELICACY VS. POWER

1647
01:02:16,332 --> 01:02:18,234
Three minutes have gone by.

1648
01:02:18,301 --> 01:02:19,869
-It's okay.
-We have enough time.

1649
01:02:19,936 --> 01:02:21,370
It's okay. We got this.

1650
01:02:21,437 --> 01:02:22,939
We got this.

1651
01:02:23,005 --> 01:02:24,407
JAPANESE CUISINE IS ALL ABOUT CUTTING!

1652
01:02:24,474 --> 01:02:25,441
Good.

1653
01:02:26,209 --> 01:02:29,278
He also isn't any less skilled
when it comes to cutting. Look.

1654
01:02:30,279 --> 01:02:31,848
He chops the zucchini peel.

1655
01:02:31,914 --> 01:02:34,083
My gosh.

1656
01:02:34,150 --> 01:02:36,152
-That's so pretty.
-He's so delicate.

1657
01:02:36,219 --> 01:02:38,121
I love the way Japanese chefs cut.

1658
01:02:38,187 --> 01:02:40,256
His fingers may look blunt,

1659
01:02:40,323 --> 01:02:41,958
but his chopping skills are delicate.

1660
01:02:43,092 --> 01:02:45,161
"His fingers may look blunt"?

1661
01:02:45,228 --> 01:02:46,662
CHEF & MY FRIDGE IS SPICY

1662
01:02:46,996 --> 01:02:48,364
3. SALT CHOPPED ZUCCHINI AND CARROT

1663
01:02:48,431 --> 01:02:51,367
Ho-young and Sam are making
two different dishes,

1664
01:02:51,434 --> 01:02:53,770
so it'd be better
if Ho-young finished as early as he can.

1665
01:02:53,836 --> 01:02:55,471
-You're right.
-That is so, right?

1666
01:02:55,538 --> 01:02:56,372
INTO THE SEAFOOD WATER!

1667
01:02:56,439 --> 01:02:58,007
What's that? Glass noodles?

1668
01:02:58,074 --> 01:02:59,442
-Thin glass noodles.
-I think

1669
01:02:59,509 --> 01:03:01,978
he's making <i>yum woon sen</i> now.

1670
01:03:02,678 --> 01:03:05,548
MOON-JUNG WANTS TO FINISH PREPPING
FOR POONG'S SAKE

1671
01:03:05,615 --> 01:03:06,749
CUCUMBER

1672
01:03:07,016 --> 01:03:08,317
CARROT

1673
01:03:08,384 --> 01:03:10,353
We'll add your favorite, carrots.

1674
01:03:10,419 --> 01:03:11,721
-Into the <i>jjajang </i>sauce?
-Yes.

1675
01:03:11,788 --> 01:03:14,657
-Can you do that?
-Because she likes them.

1676
01:03:14,724 --> 01:03:17,760
Also, the ingredients
for <i>jjajangmyeon</i> are usually diced,

1677
01:03:17,827 --> 01:03:19,262
but we're julienning everything.

1678
01:03:19,328 --> 01:03:21,497
-I see that.
-You can eat them with your noodles.

1679
01:03:21,564 --> 01:03:23,866
-Because she likes veggies.
-So she julienned them?

1680
01:03:23,933 --> 01:03:24,967
Green onion.

1681
01:03:25,034 --> 01:03:27,470
Big pieces of green onion. Good.

1682
01:03:27,537 --> 01:03:28,971
That Chinese kitchen knife is powerful.

1683
01:03:29,138 --> 01:03:30,940
THE GREEN ONION OIL!

1684
01:03:31,007 --> 01:03:32,875
-She's really fast.
-She makes it look so easy.

1685
01:03:32,942 --> 01:03:33,843
CHUCK FLAP TAIL & HANGING TENDER

1686
01:03:33,910 --> 01:03:34,977
Meat.

1687
01:03:35,044 --> 01:03:36,879
-She chops the meat quickly too.
-She's so fast.

1688
01:03:36,946 --> 01:03:37,947
And it looks so good.

1689
01:03:38,014 --> 01:03:41,150
3. CHOP AND COOK CHUCK FLAP TAIL
AND HANGING TENDER IN GREEN ONION OIL

1690
01:03:41,217 --> 01:03:42,518
Ten minutes left.

1691
01:03:42,585 --> 01:03:44,387
We decided to add tomatoes
to our <i>jjajangmyeon.</i>

1692
01:03:44,453 --> 01:03:46,289
-Really?
-It'll taste good.

1693
01:03:46,355 --> 01:03:47,924
-Have you ever had tomato <i>jjajangmyeon?</i>
-No.

1694
01:03:47,990 --> 01:03:50,426
-It's really good.
-Not even <i>doenjang jjajangmyeon.</i>

1695
01:03:52,595 --> 01:03:55,565
Moon-jung versus Ho-young.

1696
01:03:55,631 --> 01:03:56,799
A tag team round.

1697
01:03:56,866 --> 01:04:00,369
You'd better leave as much time
as you can leave for the next person.

1698
01:04:00,436 --> 01:04:05,274
He should leave at about seven and a half,
since he said he'd use six minutes.

1699
01:04:05,341 --> 01:04:08,411
-His six minutes are already up.
-Yes, it's over already.

1700
01:04:08,611 --> 01:04:10,213
CHOPPING CALMLY!

1701
01:04:10,546 --> 01:04:11,714
ANCHOVY AEKJEOT

1702
01:04:11,914 --> 01:04:13,316
GARLIC

1703
01:04:13,382 --> 01:04:15,518
What's that? Hot sauce?

1704
01:04:15,585 --> 01:04:18,054
He added anchovy <i>aekjeot,</i>
hot sauce, and garlic.

1705
01:04:18,120 --> 01:04:20,590
-The <i>yum woon sen</i> sauce.
-Is that hot sauce?

1706
01:04:20,656 --> 01:04:22,491
ALLULOSE
LOW-SUGAR OYSTER SAUCE55

1707
01:04:22,692 --> 01:04:25,561
WATER
LIME JUICE

1708
01:04:26,562 --> 01:04:27,763
-That looks good.
-Sam.

1709
01:04:27,830 --> 01:04:29,265
-Yes.
-Is it going well?

1710
01:04:29,332 --> 01:04:32,134
-Yes. He should speed up though.
-Is everything good? No? Speed up?

1711
01:04:32,201 --> 01:04:33,402
Are you adding tomatoes in there?

1712
01:04:33,469 --> 01:04:34,570
Tomatoes? Yes.

1713
01:04:34,637 --> 01:04:37,240
That's small enough.
It'll break down in the pan anyway.

1714
01:04:37,540 --> 01:04:39,175
COOK WITH MEAT!

1715
01:04:39,242 --> 01:04:42,178
Excellent! That's it!

1716
01:04:42,245 --> 01:04:43,079
THIS TIME, IT'S SMOKY!

1717
01:04:43,145 --> 01:04:46,215
Excellent! That's it!

1718
01:04:46,282 --> 01:04:47,416
That's it! Yes.

1719
01:04:47,483 --> 01:04:49,785
You're doing so great.

1720
01:04:49,852 --> 01:04:51,254
They're flustered.

1721
01:04:51,520 --> 01:04:53,389
WHO'S FLUSTERED?

1722
01:04:53,456 --> 01:04:56,525
-It's okay.
-Add them all. Everything!

1723
01:04:56,592 --> 01:04:57,760
-Everything in!
-It's nice and quiet

1724
01:04:57,827 --> 01:04:59,595
when Poong is cooking in the kitchen,

1725
01:04:59,662 --> 01:05:02,431
-but when he's not, he's so loud.
-He's doing it.

1726
01:05:02,498 --> 01:05:03,499
What an eyesore.

1727
01:05:04,734 --> 01:05:08,771
Moon-jung needs to do
as much as she can for Poong's sake.

1728
01:05:08,838 --> 01:05:10,673
Yes, I love it. She's so fast.

1729
01:05:10,740 --> 01:05:13,142
-Excellent. We have no time.
-Good job.

1730
01:05:13,209 --> 01:05:15,511
BRISKET
DROP!

1731
01:05:18,147 --> 01:05:22,485
5. ADD VEGGIES, FRIED DOENJANG,
BRISKET, FLAVORED SOY SAUCE, OYSTER SAUCE

1732
01:05:22,785 --> 01:05:24,353
Passing eight minutes.

1733
01:05:24,420 --> 01:05:25,688
What about the noodles?

1734
01:05:25,755 --> 01:05:27,556
They haven't sliced the bread either.

1735
01:05:27,623 --> 01:05:28,691
You don't have much time.

1736
01:05:28,758 --> 01:05:30,860
Will she really use 14 minutes?

1737
01:05:30,927 --> 01:05:33,195
-Excellent job.
-Eight minutes.

1738
01:05:33,763 --> 01:05:36,132
CHINESE FOOD IS ALL ABOUT SMOKINESS!

1739
01:05:36,198 --> 01:05:38,234
-Seven minutes have gone by.
-That looks awesome.

1740
01:05:38,301 --> 01:05:40,736
It smells like a Chinese restaurant.

1741
01:05:40,803 --> 01:05:42,138
It smells smoky.

1742
01:05:42,438 --> 01:05:44,573
WITCH WITH A WOK IN THE FIERY PIT!

1743
01:05:45,141 --> 01:05:47,143
-Okay. Allulose!
-Allulose.

1744
01:05:47,343 --> 01:05:49,412
ALLULOSE WILL ADD THE SWEETNESS!

1745
01:05:49,478 --> 01:05:51,747
This is so cool.

1746
01:05:52,381 --> 01:05:53,482
By their looks,

1747
01:05:53,549 --> 01:05:55,551
it feels like Ho-young would do that.

1748
01:05:55,618 --> 01:05:57,920
But he's cooking very calmly.

1749
01:05:57,987 --> 01:06:00,690
-What's that?
-I can smell the <i>jjajang</i> sauce from here.

1750
01:06:00,756 --> 01:06:03,125
You're halfway done.
Seven and a half minutes.

1751
01:06:03,192 --> 01:06:06,128
He has used more than half the time.

1752
01:06:06,195 --> 01:06:07,730
He used up a lot.

1753
01:06:07,797 --> 01:06:09,398
Sam won't have enough time.

1754
01:06:09,465 --> 01:06:12,735
-So just mix and serve? That's it?
-Yes.

1755
01:06:12,802 --> 01:06:15,604
You just have to check
if it needs more sweetness.

1756
01:06:15,671 --> 01:06:16,672
I'm done.

1757
01:06:16,973 --> 01:06:18,774
SAM KIM IS UP!

1758
01:06:18,841 --> 01:06:20,242
Now, Sam's up.

1759
01:06:20,309 --> 01:06:22,845
Sam's in, Ho-young's out.

1760
01:06:22,912 --> 01:06:23,946
They've switched.

1761
01:06:24,947 --> 01:06:26,582
Sam has to finish up.

1762
01:06:26,649 --> 01:06:27,917
Why won't your team switch?

1763
01:06:27,984 --> 01:06:29,151
Excellent.

1764
01:06:29,218 --> 01:06:31,087
He's just the cheerleader.

1765
01:06:31,153 --> 01:06:32,188
Where's the butter?

1766
01:06:32,254 --> 01:06:35,524
8. SPREAD BUTTER
ON LOW-CARB BREAD AND TOAST

1767
01:06:35,591 --> 01:06:37,259
Yes, that's it.

1768
01:06:37,326 --> 01:06:39,729
-The meat.
-Yes, the meat.

1769
01:06:40,796 --> 01:06:42,631
9. GRIND CHUCK FLAP TAIL IN A BLENDER

1770
01:06:42,698 --> 01:06:46,202
-Sam went in without much time left.
-I'm short of time.

1771
01:06:46,268 --> 01:06:47,269
I don't have much time.

1772
01:06:47,336 --> 01:06:49,939
Can he cook the meat in time?

1773
01:06:50,172 --> 01:06:51,974
BUTTON MUSHROOM, ONION

1774
01:06:52,208 --> 01:06:55,144
10. COOK BUTTON MUSHROOM,
ONION, AND CHUCK FLAP TAIL

1775
01:06:55,211 --> 01:06:56,645
Now, slice the bread for the toast.

1776
01:06:56,712 --> 01:06:59,115
HOW ARE YOU THIS BRAZEN?

1777
01:06:59,181 --> 01:07:02,151
-Moon-jung is doing everything.
-Yes, excellent job.

1778
01:07:02,218 --> 01:07:05,354
POONG SHOWS SUPPORT FROM THE SIDE!

1779
01:07:05,554 --> 01:07:08,391
SHRIMP, SCALLOPS

1780
01:07:09,325 --> 01:07:11,627
IN THE END, MOON-JUNG PREPPED
ALL THE INGREDIENTS ALONE

1781
01:07:12,728 --> 01:07:14,330
THIS IS WHY HE CHOSE MOON-JUNG!

1782
01:07:14,397 --> 01:07:17,199
Mix them. Yes.

1783
01:07:17,266 --> 01:07:19,001
Poong's cooking with his mouth.

1784
01:07:19,068 --> 01:07:20,603
You're cheating.

1785
01:07:20,669 --> 01:07:22,371
How is this cheating?

1786
01:07:22,438 --> 01:07:24,140
Can you call this a team round?

1787
01:07:24,206 --> 01:07:26,542
This is a team round. We're a team.

1788
01:07:26,609 --> 01:07:30,413
Six minutes remain,
but Poong hasn't stepped in yet.

1789
01:07:30,479 --> 01:07:33,582
-We're a team.
-Our production crew should say something.

1790
01:07:33,649 --> 01:07:35,418
-Six minutes.
-The toast is that big?

1791
01:07:35,484 --> 01:07:37,553
-She's making the toast too.
-Good job!

1792
01:07:37,620 --> 01:07:39,388
-That looks so good.
-Excellent job.

1793
01:07:39,455 --> 01:07:41,290
-Menbosha.
-The menbosha looks great.

1794
01:07:41,357 --> 01:07:43,492
When will Poong step in?

1795
01:07:43,559 --> 01:07:45,494
-Put this on top?
-Yes, on top.

1796
01:07:45,561 --> 01:07:47,663
-Isn't it too thick?
-No, it's not.

1797
01:07:47,730 --> 01:07:49,298
It's not. I'll do the rest.

1798
01:07:49,365 --> 01:07:52,234
-Will that cook in time?
-It will.

1799
01:07:52,301 --> 01:07:54,470
-Are you done? Yes? Go!
-I'm done.

1800
01:07:54,537 --> 01:07:57,039
-Go!
-Poong's going in now!

1801
01:07:57,106 --> 01:07:58,340
He's going in.

1802
01:07:58,407 --> 01:08:00,643
-Touch.
-Moon-jung, step out.

1803
01:08:00,709 --> 01:08:03,145
-Poong went in at five minutes.
-Okay.

1804
01:08:03,212 --> 01:08:04,747
-Five minutes left.
-Five minutes.

1805
01:08:04,814 --> 01:08:06,682
Please report from the kitchen, Jung-hwan.

1806
01:08:06,749 --> 01:08:08,150
Yes, the two-on-two tag team round.

1807
01:08:08,217 --> 01:08:10,453
-Is the <i>yum woon sen</i> finished?
-Yes.

1808
01:08:10,519 --> 01:08:11,554
It is.

1809
01:08:11,620 --> 01:08:12,721
Look at that.

1810
01:08:12,788 --> 01:08:14,790
-Wait a second.
-It's as big as a brick.

1811
01:08:14,857 --> 01:08:16,992
-Not that one. The heat is too low.
-Isn't the oil…

1812
01:08:17,059 --> 01:08:18,294
-…too cold?
-No.

1813
01:08:18,360 --> 01:08:20,896
He's just like me in the previous round.

1814
01:08:20,963 --> 01:08:22,531
It has to be cooked in low heat.

1815
01:08:22,598 --> 01:08:25,468
-The temperature has to be low.
-Over low heat.

1816
01:08:25,534 --> 01:08:26,769
It's on low heat.

1817
01:08:27,036 --> 01:08:29,071
SAM MAKES PHILLY CHEESE STEAK

1818
01:08:29,138 --> 01:08:30,372
-Salt.
-What?

1819
01:08:30,906 --> 01:08:32,241
EGG

1820
01:08:32,975 --> 01:08:34,944
WHAT SAUCE IS HE MAKING WITH EGG YOLK?

1821
01:08:35,277 --> 01:08:39,381
11. MIX EGG YOLK, BARBECUE SAUCE,
AND OLIVE OIL TO MAKE BARBECUE MAYO

1822
01:08:39,448 --> 01:08:41,550
-It's a mess.
-The oil won't spread.

1823
01:08:41,617 --> 01:08:43,719
He's now making chili oil.

1824
01:08:43,786 --> 01:08:47,423
She just added starch water.
Poong is practically doing nothing.

1825
01:08:47,490 --> 01:08:49,959
-Three minutes and thirty seconds!
-Okay.

1826
01:08:50,025 --> 01:08:51,627
-I don't have much time.
-It's okay.

1827
01:08:51,694 --> 01:08:53,095
Do that first and try it.

1828
01:08:53,162 --> 01:08:55,064
The roux.

1829
01:08:55,431 --> 01:08:57,766
12. COOK FLOUR IN BUTTER TO MAKE ROUX

1830
01:08:57,833 --> 01:08:59,401
AND POUR MILK TO MAKE BÉCHAMEL

1831
01:08:59,735 --> 01:09:01,937
ADDING MELTED CHEESE IN MILK!

1832
01:09:02,004 --> 01:09:02,938
He's toasting bread here.

1833
01:09:03,005 --> 01:09:05,341
-What's this sauce?
-Cheese sauce.

1834
01:09:05,407 --> 01:09:07,076
Team tag round isn't easy.

1835
01:09:07,143 --> 01:09:08,310
Mustard.

1836
01:09:08,544 --> 01:09:09,912
CARROT & ZUCCHINI RÂPÉES

1837
01:09:09,979 --> 01:09:10,913
Sugar is over there.

1838
01:09:10,980 --> 01:09:12,414
Where?

1839
01:09:12,481 --> 01:09:16,952
14. MIX ZUCCHINI & CARROTS WITH OLIVE OIL,
WHOLE GRAIN MUSTARD, PEPPER, LEMON JUICE

1840
01:09:17,386 --> 01:09:20,022
It's going into the hot oil!

1841
01:09:20,089 --> 01:09:23,826
Just like that?
At first, he cooked it over low heat

1842
01:09:23,893 --> 01:09:26,428
only to cook it once again over high heat.

1843
01:09:26,495 --> 01:09:28,697
-That looks good. Good job.
-Okay. Now, the sauce.

1844
01:09:28,764 --> 01:09:31,200
-Three minutes. You two, hands off.
-We're done.

1845
01:09:31,267 --> 01:09:33,102
-The meat.
-Ho-young, hands off.

1846
01:09:33,169 --> 01:09:34,703
Moon-jung, hands off.

1847
01:09:34,770 --> 01:09:37,106
Those two are the only ones cooking.

1848
01:09:37,673 --> 01:09:38,674
No, not yet.

1849
01:09:38,741 --> 01:09:40,176
-What's she doing?
-Hands off!

1850
01:09:40,976 --> 01:09:43,112
DROPPING THE SOYMILK NOODLES!

1851
01:09:43,179 --> 01:09:45,247
-She's cheating.
-Hands off.

1852
01:09:45,781 --> 01:09:48,484
LIKE BROTHER, LIKE SISTER

1853
01:09:49,051 --> 01:09:50,819
-Look at that.
-It's <i>jjajangmyeon.</i>

1854
01:09:51,353 --> 01:09:52,354
ADDING A JUNKY FACE

1855
01:09:52,421 --> 01:09:54,523
Jung-hwan, try the <i>jjajang </i>sauce.

1856
01:09:54,590 --> 01:09:56,091
Let's see if it tastes like <i>jjajangmyeon</i>.

1857
01:09:56,158 --> 01:09:58,394
Does it taste like <i>jjajang </i>sauce?

1858
01:09:58,460 --> 01:10:01,063
Or is it only making you judge them?

1859
01:10:03,032 --> 01:10:04,300
What's it like?

1860
01:10:05,134 --> 01:10:06,569
Did he just cough up blood?

1861
01:10:07,236 --> 01:10:08,671
Is it <i>jjajang</i> or judgy?

1862
01:10:09,338 --> 01:10:10,739
It's somewhere in between.

1863
01:10:12,808 --> 01:10:14,176
It doesn't taste like <i>jjajang.</i>

1864
01:10:14,243 --> 01:10:15,778
-What?
-Nothing like <i>jjajang?</i>

1865
01:10:15,844 --> 01:10:19,215
-It tastes like <i>gang-doenjang.</i>
-It tastes like <i>doenjang.</i>

1866
01:10:19,281 --> 01:10:21,617
Poong must've ruined it.

1867
01:10:22,851 --> 01:10:24,587
DROPPING ALLULOSE!

1868
01:10:24,653 --> 01:10:27,323
No, don't! Stop!

1869
01:10:27,389 --> 01:10:29,725
It's over. It's done.

1870
01:10:29,792 --> 01:10:31,627
-Allulose!
-It's over.

1871
01:10:31,894 --> 01:10:33,495
WE WON…

1872
01:10:33,562 --> 01:10:36,031
-But that <i>doenjang</i> is delicious.
-It's okay.

1873
01:10:36,098 --> 01:10:37,299
It's probably too sweet.

1874
01:10:39,501 --> 01:10:42,071
-Serve the râpées on the side.
-Where?

1875
01:10:43,372 --> 01:10:44,373
ALMOND SPREAD

1876
01:10:45,407 --> 01:10:46,475
COOKED CHUCK FLAP TAIL

1877
01:10:46,542 --> 01:10:48,744
Sam begins to plate the dish.

1878
01:10:48,811 --> 01:10:50,946
He's plating his dish calmly.

1879
01:10:51,013 --> 01:10:53,148
-Cheese on top. Jung-hwan!
-Yes?

1880
01:10:53,215 --> 01:10:55,417
-There's leftover meat, right?
-Yes.

1881
01:10:55,484 --> 01:10:58,153
-There's more meat.
-Is he making it runny on purpose?

1882
01:10:58,220 --> 01:10:59,088
The meat.

1883
01:10:59,154 --> 01:11:02,091
Let me try this.
One minute and ten seconds left.

1884
01:11:02,658 --> 01:11:04,860
Do you think she'll be able to know?

1885
01:11:04,927 --> 01:11:06,762
Or will she sing, "Don't Know"?

1886
01:11:06,829 --> 01:11:08,464
She will be able to know.

1887
01:11:08,530 --> 01:11:09,698
Is it good?

1888
01:11:09,765 --> 01:11:12,568
-It's great.
-It must be good.

1889
01:11:12,635 --> 01:11:14,870
I think the meat needs a bit more salt.

1890
01:11:15,804 --> 01:11:17,139
Does it taste bland?

1891
01:11:17,206 --> 01:11:18,574
Oh, but there's cheese, so it's okay.

1892
01:11:18,641 --> 01:11:20,442
The meat itself was bland.

1893
01:11:20,509 --> 01:11:22,745
One minute left.

1894
01:11:22,811 --> 01:11:24,280
Chili oil.

1895
01:11:24,346 --> 01:11:26,615
CHILI OIL EXPERT, KIM POONG!

1896
01:11:26,982 --> 01:11:28,017
Even chili oil?

1897
01:11:28,083 --> 01:11:29,385
Okay. One second.

1898
01:11:29,985 --> 01:11:32,521
-You're adding chili oil too?
-All right.

1899
01:11:32,588 --> 01:11:34,023
-Make the sauce.
-Okay.

1900
01:11:34,089 --> 01:11:35,724
Turn off the heat
to keep the noodles firm.

1901
01:11:35,791 --> 01:11:37,593
It's fine. These noodles don't get soggy.

1902
01:11:38,027 --> 01:11:39,461
SLURP

1903
01:11:39,528 --> 01:11:41,130
GOOD!

1904
01:11:41,630 --> 01:11:42,731
Lemon juice.

1905
01:11:42,798 --> 01:11:45,834
It's over there. There's the lemon.

1906
01:11:45,901 --> 01:11:47,369
-What else?
-That's it.

1907
01:11:48,037 --> 01:11:49,772
The toast. I have to get the toast.

1908
01:11:49,838 --> 01:11:52,675
The menbosha is slowly burning.

1909
01:11:52,741 --> 01:11:53,709
Is the toast burning?

1910
01:11:53,976 --> 01:11:55,311
TAKE IT OUT!

1911
01:11:55,377 --> 01:11:57,846
-Take it out now!
-Take it out!

1912
01:11:57,913 --> 01:11:59,815
MUST TAKE IT OUT!

1913
01:11:59,882 --> 01:12:02,117
-It must be hot!
-He's shaking!

1914
01:12:02,184 --> 01:12:03,819
TAKE IT OUT!

1915
01:12:03,886 --> 01:12:07,089
-Wait a second.
-He has trouble getting it out.

1916
01:12:07,156 --> 01:12:08,991
-It looks burnt!
-Oh, no.

1917
01:12:09,058 --> 01:12:10,025
It's not.

1918
01:12:10,092 --> 01:12:12,027
-It looks good.
-It's perfect.

1919
01:12:12,094 --> 01:12:14,430
It's burnt!

1920
01:12:14,496 --> 01:12:16,231
Flip it over. It's burnt!

1921
01:12:16,298 --> 01:12:17,132
Flip it over!

1922
01:12:17,199 --> 01:12:19,335
-It's burnt!
-Flip it over.

1923
01:12:19,401 --> 01:12:21,870
It's so tough to choose one.

1924
01:12:22,971 --> 01:12:24,540
ONLY HAS TO FINISH THE YUM WOON SEN

1925
01:12:24,606 --> 01:12:26,208
Where is it? In here?

1926
01:12:26,275 --> 01:12:29,044
In the right fridge. The lower part.

1927
01:12:29,111 --> 01:12:30,412
Down there. The left door.

1928
